# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson builds on the stabilizer-check language introduced in [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). It assumes you are comfortable with Pauli checks, syndromes, code distance, and the idea that surface-code checks are local on a 2D layout. A full formal treatment of stabilizer codes is available in John Watrous's [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) course on IBM Quantum&reg; Learning, but the main text only requires the working ideas summarized in the optional refresher that follows. We stay at the level of *using* the formalism rather than deriving it.\n"
msgstr "Nakabatay ang araling ito sa wika ng stabilizer-check na ipinakilala sa [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). Ipinapalagay nitong komportable ka sa mga Pauli check, syndrome, code distance, at sa ideyang lokal ang mga surface-code check sa isang 2D layout. May buong pormal na pagtalakay sa mga stabilizer code sa kursong [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) ni John Watrous sa IBM Quantum&reg; Learning, pero ang mga working idea lang na nakabuod sa opsyonal na refresher sa ibaba ang kailangan sa pangunahing teksto. Nananatili tayo sa antas ng *paggamit* ng formalism, hindi ng pagbuo nito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"New to stabilizers, or want a quick review? Open this five-minute refresher.\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Bago ka sa mga stabilizer, o gusto ng mabilis na review? Buksan ang limang-minutong refresher na ito.\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This refresher is *optional* and only reviews the minimum notation needed for correction and decoding. It is enough to follow Parts 1-6, although the more detailed sections and [Section 3.1](#bb-construction) assume more.\n"
msgstr "Ang refresher na ito ay *opsyonal* at nire-review lang ang pinakamababang notasyong kailangan para sa correction at decoding. Sapat na ito para masundan ang Parts 1-6, bagaman mas marami ang ipinapalagay ng mas detalyadong mga seksyon at ng [Section 3.1](#bb-construction).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Pauli operators.** $X$ flips $|0\\rangle \\leftrightarrow |1\\rangle$ (bit-flip), $Z$ flips the sign of $|1\\rangle$ (phase flip), and $Y = iXZ$.\n"
msgstr "**Mga Pauli operator.** Ang $X$ ay nagpapalit ng $|0\\rangle \\leftrightarrow |1\\rangle$ (bit-flip), ang $Z$ ay nagpapabaligtad ng sign ng $|1\\rangle$ (phase flip), at $Y = iXZ$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Stabilizer/check.** A multi-qubit Pauli (for example, $Z_1 Z_0$) that encoded states leave unchanged (eigenvalue $+1$). Measuring it is a *parity check*, which returns $+1$ if undisturbed and $-1$ if an error anticommuted with it, *without* measuring the encoded data itself.\n"
msgstr "**Stabilizer/check.** Isang multi-qubit Pauli (halimbawa, $Z_1 Z_0$) na hindi binabago ang mga encoded na state (eigenvalue $+1$). Ang pagsukat dito ay isang *parity check*, na nagbabalik ng $+1$ kung walang nagulo at $-1$ kung may error na nag-anticommute dito, *nang hindi* sinusukat ang mismong encoded na data.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Syndrome.** The list of $\\pm 1$ check outcomes. It is a classical bitstring carrying *partial* information about the error (not its exact location, and nothing about the encoded state). A decoder infers the most likely error from it.\n"
msgstr "**Syndrome.** Ang listahan ng mga resulta ng check na $\\pm 1$. Isa itong classical bitstring na may dalang *bahagyang* impormasyon tungkol sa error (hindi ang eksaktong lokasyon nito, at walang sinasabi tungkol sa encoded na state). Hinuhulaan ng decoder mula rito ang pinakamalamang na error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Weight.** The number of qubits an error (a multi-qubit Pauli) acts on nontrivially. For example, $Z_4X_1$ has weight 2.\n"
msgstr "**Weight.** Ang bilang ng mga qubit na kumikilos nang nontrivial ang isang error (isang multi-qubit Pauli). Halimbawa, ang $Z_4X_1$ ay may weight na 2.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Code distance $d$.** The weight of the smallest error that can slip past all checks as a *logical* error. Larger $d$ means more protection.\n"
msgstr "**Code distance $d$.** Ang weight ng pinakamaliit na error na maaaring makalusot sa lahat ng check bilang *logical* na error. Mas malaking $d$ ay mas malaking proteksyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**$[[n, k, d]]$.** $n$ physical qubits protect $k$ logical qubits with distance $d$. For example, $[[7,1,3]]$ protects one logical qubit in seven physical qubits with distance 3.\n"
msgstr "**$[[n, k, d]]$.** Pinoprotektahan ng $n$ pisikal na qubit ang $k$ logical na qubit na may distance na $d$. Halimbawa, pinoprotektahan ng $[[7,1,3]]$ ang isang logical na qubit sa pitong pisikal na qubit na may distance na 3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**CSS code.** A Calderbank-Shor-Steane (CSS) code has checks that split into pure-$X$ and pure-$Z$ types, which are handled independently. The surface code and bivariate-bicycle (BB) codes are CSS codes.\n"
msgstr "**CSS code.** Ang Calderbank-Shor-Steane (CSS) code ay may mga check na nahahati sa purong-$X$ at purong-$Z$ na uri, na hiwalay na hinahawakan. Ang surface code at ang bivariate-bicycle (BB) code ay mga CSS code.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson asks not *what* quantum error correction (QEC) is, but *when it is the right tool for quantum advantage*. To answer that, we track where the cost comes from (Parts 1 and 2), how it can be reduced (Part 3), and where it *moves* rather than vanishes (Parts 4 and 5). Then we fold all of it into the advantage judgment (Part 6).\n"
msgstr "Ang araling ito ay hindi nagtatanong kung *ano* ang quantum error correction (QEC), kundi *kailan ito ang tamang kasangkapan para sa quantum advantage*. Para masagot iyon, sinusubaybayan natin kung saan nanggagaling ang gastos (Parts 1 at 2), paano ito mababawasan (Part 3), at saan ito *lumilipat* sa halip na mawala (Parts 4 at 5). Pagkatapos, isasama natin ang lahat ng ito sa paghusga sa advantage (Part 6).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The earlier lessons introduced several ways to keep quantum computations accurate. We saw how physical errors arise and accumulate, how they can sometimes be suppressed or reshaped, and how their effect can be mitigated through additional runs and classical post-processing. The previous lesson added another idea: build checks into the computation so that a corrupted run can be identified and discarded. Its code-based syndrome post-selection, including spacetime checks, is a form of post-selected quantum error correction ([Qiskit Paulice blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qiskit-paulice)). This improves the quality of the retained data, but it also leaves limitations. Some errors pass undetected, a triggered check might reveal that something went wrong without identifying the most likely fault, and the method is most effective for circuits whose structure supports low-overhead checks. Decoding-based correction builds on the same check information. Instead of asking whether a run should be kept or discarded, it asks which error is most consistent with the observed pattern and how the encoded information should be recovered. The distinction here is between syndrome-based post-selection and decoding with recovery, two approaches along the same QEC continuum ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)). That extra power changes the resource trade-off. Decoding-based correction shifts more of the cost toward physical qubits, syndrome extraction, decoding, connectivity, and architecture. This trades sampling cost for additional qubits and hardware resources along the QEC continuum ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)), becoming central when lighter-weight methods cannot support the depth or reliability a task requires.\n"
msgstr "Ipinakilala ng mga naunang aralin ang ilang paraan para panatilihing tumpak ang mga quantum computation. Nakita natin kung paano lumilitaw at naiipon ang mga pisikal na error, kung paano minsang masusupil o mahuhubog muli ang mga ito, at kung paano mababawasan ang epekto nila sa pamamagitan ng karagdagang mga run at classical post-processing. Nagdagdag ang nakaraang aralin ng isa pang ideya: bumuo ng mga check sa loob ng computation para matukoy at maitapon ang isang sirang run. Ang code-based syndrome post-selection nito, kasama ang mga spacetime check, ay isang uri ng post-selected quantum error correction ([Qiskit Paulice blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qiskit-paulice)). Pinapabuti nito ang kalidad ng mga datos na pinanatili, pero may mga limitasyon pa rin. May mga error na hindi natutukoy, ang isang na-trigger na check ay maaaring magpakitang may nagkamali pero hindi tinutukoy ang pinakamalamang na fault, at pinakamabisa ang paraang ito para sa mga circuit na ang istruktura ay sumusuporta sa mga check na mababa ang overhead. Nakabatay ang decoding-based correction sa parehong impormasyon ng check. Sa halip na itanong kung dapat bang panatilihin o itapon ang isang run, itinatanong nito kung aling error ang pinakaangkop sa naobserbahang pattern at kung paano dapat mabawi ang encoded na impormasyon. Ang pagkakaiba rito ay sa pagitan ng syndrome-based post-selection at decoding na may recovery, dalawang diskarte sa iisang QEC continuum ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)). Binabago ng karagdagang kakayahang iyon ang trade-off sa resource. Inililipat ng decoding-based correction ang mas malaking bahagi ng gastos sa mga pisikal na qubit, syndrome extraction, decoding, connectivity, at arkitektura. Pinapalitan nito ang sampling cost ng karagdagang mga qubit at hardware resource sa kahabaan ng QEC continuum ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)), at nagiging sentral kapag hindi na kayang suportahan ng mas magagaan na paraan ang lalim o pagiging maaasahang kailangan ng isang gawain.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This lesson begins by connecting decoding and recovery to the post-selection methods of the previous lesson, then turns to the most established way to protect stored quantum information and why it is so expensive. From there it asks how that cost might be reduced and examines a concrete recent proposal as a case study. It then explains why protecting stored information is not enough for useful computation and what additional resources a full algorithm needs. Next, it steps back to view error correction as a whole system, including decoders, modular architecture, and the different challenges that other hardware platforms inherit. The lesson closes on the central question of [Lanes et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), which asks when error correction helps preserve a quantum advantage and when its overhead erases it.\n"
msgstr "Sinisimulan ng araling ito sa pag-uugnay ng decoding at recovery sa mga post-selection na paraan ng nakaraang aralin, saka tumutungo sa pinakamatatag na paraan para protektahan ang nakaimbak na quantum na impormasyon at kung bakit ito napakamahal. Mula roon, itinatanong nito kung paano mababawasan ang gastos na iyon at sinusuri ang isang konkretong kamakailang panukala bilang case study. Ipinapaliwanag nito pagkatapos kung bakit hindi sapat ang pagprotekta sa nakaimbak na impormasyon para sa kapaki-pakinabang na computation at anong karagdagang resource ang kailangan ng isang buong algorithm. Kasunod, umaatras ito para tingnan ang error correction bilang isang buong sistema, kasama ang mga decoder, modular na arkitektura, at ang iba't ibang hamong namamana ng ibang hardware platform. Nagtatapos ang aralin sa sentral na tanong ng [Lanes et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), na nagtatanong kung kailan nakakatulong ang error correction na mapanatili ang quantum advantage at kailan binubura ito ng overhead nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"A note on scope\">\n"
msgstr "<Admonition type=\"note\" title=\"Tala tungkol sa saklaw\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Quantum error correction is a problem for every hardware platform, but the form it takes differs. This lesson uses recent IBM&reg; work on bivariate-bicycle and gross-code architectures as a concrete case study, while keeping the main framing from [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658). Within that scope, we focus on *stabilizer codes on 2D superconducting transmons* (a *transmon* is the most common type of superconducting qubit), where the design challenge is to lay out checks on a flat grid and absorb non-local connectivity through modular hardware. Trapped-ion, neutral-atom, bosonic-mode, and topological-qubit platforms inherit different challenges, which we return to in Part 5. Until then, \"physical qubit\", \"stabilizer check\", and \"2D layout\" should be read as describing the superconducting setting.\n"
msgstr "Ang quantum error correction ay problema ng bawat hardware platform, pero iba-iba ang anyo nito. Gumagamit ang araling ito ng kamakailang gawa ng IBM&reg; sa mga bivariate-bicycle at gross-code na arkitektura bilang konkretong case study, habang pinapanatili ang pangunahing balangkas mula sa [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658). Sa saklaw na iyon, nakatuon tayo sa *mga stabilizer code sa 2D superconducting transmon* (ang *transmon* ay ang pinakakaraniwang uri ng superconducting qubit), kung saan ang hamon sa disenyo ay ilatag ang mga check sa patag na grid at sipsipin ang non-local na connectivity sa pamamagitan ng modular na hardware. Ang mga platform na trapped-ion, neutral-atom, bosonic-mode, at topological-qubit ay may iba't ibang hamon, na babalikan natin sa Part 5. Hanggang doon, ang \"physical qubit\", \"stabilizer check\", at \"2D layout\" ay dapat unawain bilang naglalarawan sa superconducting na setting.\n"

