#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We begin by importing the libraries used throughout the lesson.\n"
msgstr "Sisimulan natin sa pag-import ng mga library na ginagamit sa buong aralin.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 1: From detection to correction with stabilizer codes for error recovery {#part-1-from-detection-to-correction-with-stabilizer-codes-for-error-recovery}"
msgstr "Part 1: Mula detection tungo sa correction gamit ang mga stabilizer code para sa error recovery {#part-1-from-detection-to-correction-with-stabilizer-codes-for-error-recovery}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This part makes the relationship between detection and correction concrete. The check-based language and the notion of code distance are already familiar from the previous lesson, so we recall them briefly and look at how correction uses the same information more fully. This is the first entry in the lesson's running cost accounting. Correction buys more than detection, and the price appears first in the code distance it demands. Detecting a weight-$w$ error (one acting nontrivially on $w$ qubits) needs $d \\ge w+1$, while correcting it needs $d \\ge 2w+1$.\n"
"<span id=\"recap\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Ginagawang konkreto ng bahaging ito ang ugnayan ng detection at correction. Pamilyar na mula sa nakaraang aralin ang wika na nakabatay sa check at ang konsepto ng code distance, kaya sandali lang natin silang babalikan at titingnan kung paano mas ganap na ginagamit ng correction ang parehong impormasyon. Ito ang unang entry sa tuloy-tuloy na pagtutuos ng gastos sa aralin. Mas marami ang nabibili ng correction kaysa sa detection, at lumilitaw muna ang presyo sa code distance na hinihingi nito. Ang pag-detect ng weight-$w$ na error (isang kumikilos nang nontrivial sa $w$ na qubit) ay nangangailangan ng $d \\ge w+1$, habang ang pag-correct dito ay nangangailangan ng $d \\ge 2w+1$.\n"
"<span id=\"recap\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1 Recap and what changes for correction {#11-recap-and-what-changes-for-correction}"
msgstr "1.1 Recap at kung ano ang nagbabago para sa correction {#11-recap-and-what-changes-for-correction}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The check-based language we use in this section is not new. [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i) already worked with stabilizer checks and their syndromes when introducing the $[[4,2,2]]$ detection code, and code distance appeared there as the smallest error weight a code fails to detect.\n"
msgstr "Hindi bago ang wikang nakabatay sa check na ginagamit natin sa seksyong ito. Gumamit na ang [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i) ng mga stabilizer check at ng kanilang mga syndrome nang ipakilala ang $[[4,2,2]]$ detection code, at lumitaw doon ang code distance bilang pinakamaliit na error weight na hindi na-didetect ng isang code.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"What changes in correction is how these objects are used. The same checks now return a *syndrome* that must do more than confirm something went wrong. It has to narrow the possibilities enough for a decoder to choose a likely recovery. The same code distance $d$ also plays a stronger role. A code of distance $d$ can correct any error of weight up to $\\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$, which is roughly twice as demanding as detection alone (detecting a weight-$w$ error needs $d \\ge w+1$, while correcting it needs $d \\ge 2w+1$). These two upgrades, informative syndromes and a stricter use of distance, separate correction from the detection step of the previous lesson, and they set up the cost we examine in later sections.\n"
"<span id=\"repetition-code\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang nagbabago sa correction ay kung paano ginagamit ang mga bagay na ito. Ang parehong mga check ay nagbabalik na ngayon ng *syndrome* na dapat gumawa ng higit pa sa pagkumpirmang may nagkamali. Dapat nitong paliitin ang mga posibilidad hanggang sapat para makapili ang decoder ng malamang na recovery. Mas malakas din ang papel ng parehong code distance na $d$. Ang code na may distance na $d$ ay kayang mag-correct ng anumang error na may weight hanggang $\\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$, na halos dalawang beses na mas mahirap kaysa sa detection lang (ang pag-detect ng weight-$w$ na error ay nangangailangan ng $d \\ge w+1$, habang ang pag-correct dito ay nangangailangan ng $d \\ge 2w+1$). Ang dalawang pag-upgrade na ito, ang mga informative na syndrome at ang mas mahigpit na paggamit ng distance, ang naghihiwalay sa correction mula sa detection step ng nakaraang aralin, at inihahanda nila ang gastos na susuriin natin sa mga susunod na seksyon.\n"
"<span id=\"repetition-code\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.2 A worked example: the three-qubit repetition code {#12-a-worked-example-the-three-qubit-repetition-code}"
msgstr "1.2 Isang halimbawang gawa: ang three-qubit repetition code {#12-a-worked-example-the-three-qubit-repetition-code}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The simplest example of correction-as-decoding is the three-qubit repetition code. One logical qubit, protected only against bit-flips, is encoded into three physical qubits, and a single bit-flip can be inferred from two parity checks, $Z_1 Z_0$ and $Z_2 Z_1$. The syndrome does more than signal that something went wrong. It indicates which qubit most likely flipped, so the logical value can be recovered by majority vote.\n"
msgstr "Ang pinakasimpleng halimbawa ng correction-as-decoding ay ang three-qubit repetition code. Isang logical na qubit, na protektado lang laban sa mga bit-flip, ay ine-encode sa tatlong pisikal na qubit, at ang isang bit-flip ay mahihinuha mula sa dalawang parity check, $Z_1 Z_0$ at $Z_2 Z_1$. Higit pa sa pagsenyas na may nagkamali ang ginagawa ng syndrome. Ipinapakita nito kung aling qubit ang malamang na nag-flip, kaya mababawi ang logical na value sa pamamagitan ng majority vote.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We use Watrous's [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) course as the reference for the encoding circuit, stabilizer picture, syndrome table, and logical error curve, rather than re-deriving them in full here. As that course emphasizes, the repetition code is only a limited quantum code. It protects against bit-flips but not against phase errors, so it is best viewed as a pedagogical starting point only.\n"
msgstr "Ginagamit natin ang kursong [Foundations of quantum error correction](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/) ni Watrous bilang reference para sa encoding circuit, stabilizer picture, syndrome table, at logical error curve, sa halip na buuin muli ang mga ito nang buo rito. Gaya ng idiniin ng kursong iyon, limitado lang na quantum code ang repetition code. Pinoprotektahan nito laban sa mga bit-flip pero hindi sa mga phase error, kaya pinakamainam itong ituring na panimulang punto lamang para sa pagtuturo.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two ideas from this example carry forward. First, the syndrome is *informative*, supporting decoding rather than just filtering. This is the step from detection to correction described in [Section 1.1](#recap). Second, the logical error rate improves only when the physical error rate is below a threshold-like crossover. For the three-bit repetition code, the decoded error rate $3p^2 - 2p^3$ is lower than the unencoded error rate $p$ only when $p < 1/2$. This simple picture foreshadows the more important threshold behavior that appears again in later sections, especially for the surface code in Part 2.\n"
msgstr "Dalawang ideya mula sa halimbawang ito ang dadalhin natin pasulong. Una, *informative* ang syndrome, na sumusuporta sa decoding sa halip na pagsala lang. Ito ang hakbang mula detection tungo sa correction na inilarawan sa [Section 1.1](#recap). Pangalawa, bumubuti lang ang logical error rate kapag mas mababa ang pisikal na error rate kaysa sa isang threshold-like na crossover. Para sa three-bit repetition code, mas mababa ang decoded error rate na $3p^2 - 2p^3$ kaysa sa unencoded error rate na $p$ kapag $p < 1/2$ lang. Ang simpleng larawang ito ay nagbabadya ng mas mahalagang threshold behavior na muling lilitaw sa mga susunod na seksyon, lalo na para sa surface code sa Part 2.\n"

