#, no-wrap
msgid "*Encoding circuit for the three-qubit repetition code. Source: Watrous, [Foundations of quantum error correction, Repetition codes](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/repetition-codes), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"
msgstr "*Encoding circuit para sa three-qubit repetition code. Pinagmulan: Watrous, [Foundations of quantum error correction, Repetition codes](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/repetition-codes), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As the table shows, each single-qubit bit-flip produces a distinct syndrome, so the syndrome alone tells the decoder which qubit to flip back.\n"
msgstr "Gaya ng ipinapakita ng talahanayan, bawat single-qubit bit-flip ay gumagawa ng natatanging syndrome, kaya sapat na ang syndrome para sabihin sa decoder kung aling qubit ang ibabalik.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/extracted-outputs/36475b64-0.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/extracted-outputs/36475b64-0.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The encoded error rate of the three-bit repetition code is $3p^2 - 2p^3$. Majority vote fails when at least two of the three qubits flip, so the failure probability is $\\binom{3}{2}p^2(1-p) + \\binom{3}{3}p^3 = 3p^2 - 2p^3$. Plotting it against the unencoded rate $p$ makes the crossover at $p = 1/2$ explicit. The same visual logic (*encoded error below unencoded error below a crossover*) reappears for the surface code in Part 2, where the crossover becomes the code threshold and the physical error rate of interest sits far below it.\n"
msgstr "Ang encoded error rate ng three-bit repetition code ay $3p^2 - 2p^3$. Pumapalya ang majority vote kapag hindi bababa sa dalawa sa tatlong qubit ang nag-flip, kaya ang probability ng pagkabigo ay $\\binom{3}{2}p^2(1-p) + \\binom{3}{3}p^3 = 3p^2 - 2p^3$. Ang pag-plot nito laban sa unencoded rate na $p$ ay nagpapalinaw sa crossover sa $p = 1/2$. Ang parehong visual na lohika (*encoded error na mas mababa sa unencoded error na mas mababa sa isang crossover*) ay muling lilitaw para sa surface code sa Part 2, kung saan ang crossover ay nagiging code threshold at ang pisikal na error rate na pinag-uukulan ay nasa ilalim nito nang malayo.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This plot also explains when correction is worth its cost. For shallow circuits, the advantage is small, and the lighter-weight methods from earlier lessons (suppression, mitigation, and detection-and-discard) are often enough to keep results meaningful. The situation changes at depth. At larger circuit volumes, rigorous mitigation methods can require rapidly growing (often exponential) sampling overhead, and post-selected detection retains a shrinking fraction of runs. This applies to deep, general-purpose fault-tolerant algorithms, such as Shor's factoring algorithm, phase estimation, and the deep chemistry routines built on it. Correction becomes important there because it pays a large architectural overhead in physical qubits, decoding, and connectivity in exchange for an error rate that scales gently with depth. In the framework of [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), near-term advantage claims might still rely on mitigation or post-selected detection where the depth and validation requirements allow. Decoding-based correction becomes central when those methods cannot provide rigorous error control at the required depth, while mitigation and post-selection can continue to complement it.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapaliwanag din ng plot na ito kung kailan sulit ang gastos ng correction. Para sa mga mababaw na circuit, maliit ang advantage, at kadalasang sapat na ang mga mas magagaan na paraan mula sa mga naunang aralin (suppression, mitigation, at detection-and-discard) para manatiling makabuluhan ang mga resulta. Nagbabago ang sitwasyon sa lalim. Sa mas malalaking circuit volume, ang mga mahigpit na mitigation method ay maaaring mangailangan ng mabilis na lumalaking (madalas na exponential) sampling overhead, at ang post-selected detection ay nagpapanatili ng papaliit na bahagi ng mga run. Nalalapat ito sa malalalim at pangkalahatang fault-tolerant na algorithm, tulad ng factoring algorithm ni Shor, phase estimation, at ng malalalim na chemistry routine na nakabatay dito. Nagiging mahalaga roon ang correction dahil nagbabayad ito ng malaking architectural overhead sa mga pisikal na qubit, decoding, at connectivity kapalit ng error rate na dahan-dahan lang lumalaki kasabay ng lalim. Sa balangkas ng [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), maaaring umasa pa rin ang mga near-term na claim ng advantage sa mitigation o post-selected detection kung saan pinapayagan ng lalim at mga kinakailangan sa validation. Nagiging sentral ang decoding-based correction kapag hindi kayang magbigay ng mga paraang iyon ng mahigpit na error control sa kinakailangang lalim, habang maaaring magpatuloy ang mitigation at post-selection na umagapay dito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "Tingnan ang iyong pang-unawa {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Is a syndrome a quantum state or a classical bitstring, and what does it tell you?\n"
msgstr "Ang syndrome ba ay quantum state o classical bitstring, at ano ang sinasabi nito sa iyo?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Sagot\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A syndrome is a classical bitstring, the list of $\\pm 1$ check outcomes. It carries partial information about the *error* (not about the encoded logical state, and without collapsing it). A decoder then infers the most likely error from it.\n"
msgstr "Ang syndrome ay isang classical bitstring, ang listahan ng mga resulta ng check na $\\pm 1$. May dala itong bahagyang impormasyon tungkol sa *error* (hindi tungkol sa encoded na logical state, at hindi ito nako-collapse). Hinuhulaan naman ng decoder mula rito ang pinakamalamang na error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Given a stabilizer code $[[n,k,d]]$, how do you tell whether it is used for error *detection* or error *correction*? Work out the detectable and correctable error weights for $[[4,2,2]]$, the Steane code $[[7,1,3]]$ (a seven-qubit CSS code holding one logical qubit at distance 3), and the gross code $[[144,12,12]]$.\n"
msgstr "Sa isang stabilizer code na $[[n,k,d]]$, paano mo malalaman kung ginagamit ito para sa error *detection* o error *correction*? Alamin ang mga detectable at correctable na error weight para sa $[[4,2,2]]$, ang Steane code na $[[7,1,3]]$ (isang seven-qubit CSS code na may hawak na isang logical na qubit sa distance na 3), at ang gross code na $[[144,12,12]]$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The same code can do either. Which one you get is a choice of how you process the syndrome, not a property built into the code. Detection works for any error of weight $\\le d-1$, and correction works for weight $\\le \\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$ (from $d \\ge w+1$ to detect, $d \\ge 2w+1$ to correct a weight-$w$ error).\n"
msgstr "Kayang gawin ng parehong code ang alinman. Pagpili kung paano mo pinoproseso ang syndrome ang nagtatakda kung alin ang makukuha mo, hindi isang katangiang nakapaloob sa code. Gumagana ang detection para sa anumang error na may weight na $\\le d-1$, at gumagana ang correction para sa weight na $\\le \\lfloor (d-1)/2 \\rfloor$ (mula sa $d \\ge w+1$ para mag-detect, $d \\ge 2w+1$ para mag-correct ng weight-$w$ na error).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| code | $d$ | detect up to | correct up to |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| $[[4,2,2]]$ (previous lesson) | 2 | 1 | 0 |\n"
"| Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n"
"| gross $[[144,12,12]]$ | 12 | 11 | 5 |\n"
msgstr ""
"| code | $d$ | detect hanggang | correct hanggang |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| $[[4,2,2]]$ (nakaraang aralin) | 2 | 1 | 0 |\n"
"| Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ | 3 | 2 | 1 |\n"
"| gross $[[144,12,12]]$ | 12 | 11 | 5 |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is why $[[4,2,2]]$ was detection-only in [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). With $d=2$, the correction radius is 0. (Avoid two common errors. The *parity* of $d$ is irrelevant, and correction already works at $d=3$, not only at $d \\ge 4$.)\n"
msgstr "Ito ang dahilan kung bakit detection-only ang $[[4,2,2]]$ sa [Error correction I](/learning/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i). Sa $d=2$, 0 ang correction radius. (Iwasan ang dalawang karaniwang pagkakamali. Walang kinalaman ang *parity* ng $d$, at gumagana na ang correction sa $d=3$, hindi lang sa $d \\ge 4$.)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The bounds here ($d \\ge w+1$ to detect, $d \\ge 2w+1$ to correct) are the code's **combinatorial distance**, assuming syndrome measurement is itself noiseless. In a real fault-tolerant setting, the syndrome-extraction circuit is also faulty, so recovery is governed not by $d$ alone but by the circuit-level distance and threshold under repeated noisy measurement. We use this realistic model starting in Part 3.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga hangganan dito ($d \\ge w+1$ para mag-detect, $d \\ge 2w+1$ para mag-correct) ay ang **combinatorial distance** ng code, na ipinapalagay na walang noise ang syndrome measurement mismo. Sa totoong fault-tolerant na setting, may fault din ang syndrome-extraction circuit, kaya ang recovery ay hindi pinamamahalaan ng $d$ lang kundi ng circuit-level distance at threshold sa ilalim ng paulit-ulit na noisy na measurement. Gagamitin natin ang makatotohanang modelong ito simula sa Part 3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 2: Two-dimensional stabilizer codes and the locality-rate-distance trade-off {#part-2-two-dimensional-stabilizer-codes-and-the-locality-rate-distance-trade-off}"
msgstr "Part 2: Mga two-dimensional na stabilizer code at ang locality-rate-distance na trade-off {#part-2-two-dimensional-stabilizer-codes-and-the-locality-rate-distance-trade-off}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two choices frame the trade-off in this part. The surface code uses *local* checks, which simplifies hardware implementation, but the overhead grows with scale. Quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes use *sparse non-local* checks, which can shrink the overhead but make the hardware and decoder harder. The rest of this part is about *why* that trade-off exists. The surface code is among the most studied quantum error-correcting codes, and it is a natural starting point for any discussion of correction overhead. Two features make it the reference baseline in the superconducting setting. Its stabilizer checks act between nearest neighbors on a two-dimensional grid, matching the connectivity of leading hardware, and it has a comparatively high noise threshold, so a useful logical error rate can be reached at physical error rates that are realistic in practice. This part introduces the surface code at the level needed to discuss its cost, then asks where that cost comes from structurally, and finally surveys the broader landscape of codes that relax the strict 2D-locality assumption. The reason this cost is hard to avoid is structural. For any code whose checks act only between nearest neighbors on a 2D grid, the dimensionless ratio $kd^2/n$ is capped at a constant, called the Bravyi-Poulin-Terhal (BPT) ceiling. That ceiling holds the advantage back, as [Section 2.3](#bpt-bound) develops.\n"
msgstr "Dalawang pagpipilian ang bumubuo sa trade-off sa bahaging ito. Gumagamit ang surface code ng mga *lokal* na check, na nagpapasimple sa hardware implementation, pero lumalaki ang overhead habang lumalaki ang sukat. Gumagamit ang mga quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) code ng mga *sparse na non-local* na check, na maaaring magpaliit ng overhead pero nagpapahirap sa hardware at decoder. Ang natitirang bahagi nito ay tungkol sa *kung bakit* umiiral ang trade-off na ito. Ang surface code ay isa sa mga pinakapinag-aralang quantum error-correcting code, at natural itong panimulang punto sa anumang talakayan ng correction overhead. Dalawang katangian ang gumagawa rito bilang reference baseline sa superconducting na setting. Kumikilos ang mga stabilizer check nito sa pagitan ng magkakalapit na kapitbahay sa two-dimensional na grid, na tugma sa connectivity ng mga nangungunang hardware, at may medyo mataas itong noise threshold, kaya makakamit ang kapaki-pakinabang na logical error rate sa mga pisikal na error rate na makatotohanan sa praktika. Ipinakikilala ng bahaging ito ang surface code sa antas na kailangan para talakayin ang gastos nito, saka itinatanong kung saan nanggagaling ang gastos na iyon sa istruktura, at sa huli ay sinusuri ang mas malawak na tanawin ng mga code na lumuluwag sa mahigpit na 2D-locality na pagpapalagay. Istruktural ang dahilan kung bakit mahirap iwasan ang gastos na ito. Para sa anumang code na ang mga check ay kumikilos lang sa pagitan ng magkakalapit na kapitbahay sa 2D grid, may hangganan sa isang constant ang dimensionless na ratio na $kd^2/n$, na tinatawag na Bravyi-Poulin-Terhal (BPT) ceiling. Pinipigilan ng ceiling na iyon ang advantage, gaya ng inilalahad sa [Section 2.3](#bpt-bound).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.1 Stabilizers on a 2D lattice {#21-stabilizers-on-a-2d-lattice}"
msgstr "2.1 Mga stabilizer sa isang 2D lattice {#21-stabilizers-on-a-2d-lattice}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The surface code composes stabilizer checks into a two-dimensional grid. Data qubits sit on the edges of a square lattice, with ancilla qubits on the faces and at the vertices. Each ancilla measures a *weight-4 stabilizer* (either an X-type around a vertex or a Z-type around a face) on the four data qubits adjacent to it, using only nearest-neighbor two-qubit gates. The full syndrome-extraction circuit fits on a flat layout, which aligns the surface code with planar superconducting hardware.\n"
msgstr "Binubuo ng surface code ang mga stabilizer check sa isang two-dimensional na grid. Ang mga data qubit ay nasa mga gilid ng isang square lattice, at ang mga ancilla qubit ay nasa mga mukha at sa mga vertex. Bawat ancilla ay sumusukat ng isang *weight-4 stabilizer* (alinman sa X-type sa paligid ng vertex o Z-type sa paligid ng mukha) sa apat na data qubit na katabi nito, gamit lang ang nearest-neighbor na two-qubit gate. Kasya ang buong syndrome-extraction circuit sa patag na layout, kaya tugma ang surface code sa planar na superconducting hardware.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Weight-4 stabilizer generators of the toric/surface code: X-type around a vertex, Z-type around a face](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-stabilizer-generators.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Weight-4 stabilizer generators of the toric/surface code: X-type around a vertex, Z-type around a face](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code-stabilizer-generators.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Two stabilizer generator types of the toric/surface code, each of weight 4. Source: [Foundations of quantum error correction, Toric code](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"
msgstr "*Dalawang uri ng stabilizer generator ng toric/surface code, bawat isa ay may weight na 4. Pinagmulan: [Foundations of quantum error correction, Toric code](/learning/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/toric-code), IBM Quantum Learning.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two further properties make the surface code the reference toward later code families. The first is its noise *threshold*. Under realistic circuit-level noise, encoding helps once physical error rates fall below roughly one percent ([Fowler et al., 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.0928)), a level often quoted near the one-percent scale, which is one reason the surface code has become the baseline for superconducting architectures. The second is the maturity of its *decoding*. Under standard Pauli-noise assumptions, inferring the most likely error from a syndrome is often formulated as a *minimum-weight matching* problem on syndrome endpoints on the lattice, a problem solved by efficient classical algorithms.\n"
msgstr "Dalawa pang katangian ang gumagawa sa surface code na reference tungo sa mga susunod na pamilya ng code. Ang una ay ang noise *threshold* nito. Sa ilalim ng makatotohanang circuit-level na noise, nakakatulong ang encoding kapag bumaba ang mga pisikal na error rate sa humigit-kumulang isang porsyento ([Fowler et al., 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.0928)), isang antas na madalas banggitin sa paligid ng isang porsyento, na isa sa mga dahilan kung bakit naging baseline ang surface code para sa mga superconducting na arkitektura. Ang pangalawa ay ang pagkahinog ng *decoding* nito. Sa ilalim ng karaniwang Pauli-noise na mga pagpapalagay, ang paghihinuha ng pinakamalamang na error mula sa syndrome ay madalas na binubuo bilang problema ng *minimum-weight matching* sa mga syndrome endpoint sa lattice, isang problemang nalulutas ng mga episyenteng classical algorithm.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Minimum-weight matching corrections on the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-correction.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Minimum-weight matching corrections on the toric code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/quantum-code-constructions/X-error-correction.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*A pair of syndrome endpoints (gray tiles) connected by a shortest-path correction (yellow). Decoding the surface code reduces to minimum-weight matching of such endpoints. Source: same lesson.*\n"
msgstr "*Isang pares ng syndrome endpoint (gray na tile) na pinag-ugnay ng shortest-path na correction (dilaw). Ang pag-decode sa surface code ay nauuwi sa minimum-weight matching ng mga ganoong endpoint. Pinagmulan: parehong aralin.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The figure adds the part the syndrome leaves out. Because only the endpoints are visible, the decoder cannot see the actual error chain (magenta). It can only choose a chain with the same endpoints, and minimum-weight matching picks the shortest one (yellow) in this example.\n"
msgstr "Idinaragdag ng figure ang bahaging iniiwan ng syndrome. Dahil ang mga endpoint lang ang nakikita, hindi nakikita ng decoder ang aktwal na error chain (magenta). Makakapili lang ito ng chain na may parehong mga endpoint, at ang minimum-weight matching ay pumipili ng pinakamaikli (dilaw) sa halimbawang ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The lattice geometry also changes what a logical operator looks like. In the $[[4,2,2]]$ code from the previous lesson, logical operators were short Pauli strings of weight equal to the distance. Here they are *string operators*, which are chains of single-qubit Paulis along a path in the lattice. A short chain of errors creates a string with two endpoints, and the stabilizers at those endpoints fire. The syndrome reveals *where* the chain ends, even though the chain itself is not directly observed. Seeing only the endpoints, the decoder takes the shortest chain joining them (yellow), which is not necessarily the real one. That is fine. The actual errors and the correction together close a loop, and a loop that shrinks to a point is a product of stabilizers, so the qubit is recovered even when the guessed path is \"wrong\". A logical error, by contrast, is a string with no endpoints. In the toric code, it wraps around the torus, while in the open-boundary surface code we focus on, it stretches from one boundary of the patch to the other. The distance $d$ is the length of the shortest such nontrivial string, and the number of physical qubits in a distance-$d$ patch scales as $d^2$.\n"
"<span id=\"surface-overhead\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Binabago rin ng geometry ng lattice kung ano ang hitsura ng isang logical operator. Sa $[[4,2,2]]$ code mula sa nakaraang aralin, ang mga logical operator ay maiikling Pauli string na may weight na katumbas ng distance. Dito, ang mga ito ay mga *string operator*, na mga chain ng single-qubit Pauli sa isang landas sa lattice. Ang isang maikling chain ng mga error ay lumilikha ng string na may dalawang endpoint, at nagfa-fire ang mga stabilizer sa mga endpoint na iyon. Ipinapakita ng syndrome kung *saan* nagtatapos ang chain, kahit hindi direktang naoobserbahan ang mismong chain. Dahil mga endpoint lang ang nakikita, kinukuha ng decoder ang pinakamaikling chain na nag-uugnay sa kanila (dilaw), na hindi kinakailangang ang totoo. Ayos lang iyon. Ang aktwal na mga error at ang correction ay magkasamang bumubuo ng loop, at ang loop na lumiliit hanggang isang punto ay produkto ng mga stabilizer, kaya nababawi ang qubit kahit \"mali\" ang hinulaang landas. Sa kabilang banda, ang logical error ay isang string na walang endpoint. Sa toric code, bumabalot ito sa torus, habang sa open-boundary na surface code na pinagtutuunan natin, umaabot ito mula sa isang hangganan ng patch hanggang sa kabila. Ang distance na $d$ ay ang haba ng pinakamaikling ganoong nontrivial na string, at ang bilang ng mga pisikal na qubit sa isang distance-$d$ na patch ay sumusukat bilang $d^2$.\n"
"<span id=\"surface-overhead\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.2 Why 2D locality creates overhead {#22-why-2d-locality-creates-overhead}"
msgstr "2.2 Bakit lumilikha ng overhead ang 2D locality {#22-why-2d-locality-creates-overhead}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Being below threshold is a necessary condition, not a sufficient one. To reach a *target* logical error rate (say, $10^{-12}$ per round, a demanding stress-test target for deep fault-tolerant algorithms), the code distance has to grow until the residual logical-error probability drops to that level. The standard surface-code scaling has the per-round logical error behaving as:\n"
msgstr "Ang pagiging mas mababa sa threshold ay kinakailangang kondisyon, hindi sapat. Para maabot ang isang *target* na logical error rate (halimbawa, $10^{-12}$ kada round, isang mahigpit na stress-test na target para sa malalalim na fault-tolerant algorithm), kailangang lumaki ang code distance hanggang bumaba ang natitirang logical-error probability sa antas na iyon. Ang karaniwang surface-code scaling ay may per-round na logical error na kumikilos bilang:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\varepsilon_L(d, p) \\;\\approx\\; A\\left(\\frac{p}{p_{\\text{th}}}\\right)^{(d+1)/2},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\varepsilon_L(d, p) \\;\\approx\\; A\\left(\\frac{p}{p_{\\text{th}}}\\right)^{(d+1)/2},\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "with threshold $p_{\\text{th}} \\sim 10^{-2}$ and a code-dependent prefactor $A$ of order $0.1$. The exponent $(d+1)/2$ says that each two extra units of distance gain roughly a factor $p / p_{\\text{th}}$ in logical error, so well below threshold, and the suppression is exponential in $d$, but only in $d$, not in qubit count ([Fowler and Gidney, 2018](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.06709)).\n"
msgstr "na may threshold na $p_{\\text{th}} \\sim 10^{-2}$ at isang code-dependent na prefactor na $A$ na nasa order na $0.1$. Ang exponent na $(d+1)/2$ ay nagsasabing bawat dalawang dagdag na unit ng distance ay nakakakuha ng humigit-kumulang na factor na $p / p_{\\text{th}}$ sa logical error, kaya malayo sa ilalim ng threshold, at exponential ang suppression sa $d$, pero sa $d$ lang, hindi sa bilang ng qubit ([Fowler and Gidney, 2018](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.06709)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The qubit count grows quadratically. A bare distance-$d$ rotated patch uses $d^2$ data qubits and $d^2-1$ syndrome qubits, totaling $2d^2-1$ physical qubits per logical qubit ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7)). Combining the two scalings, reaching a deep target error rate forces $d$ to grow logarithmically with the target while the physical-qubit footprint grows as the square of that. The plot below makes the trade-off explicit. At a physical error rate of $p = 10^{-3}$, a reasonable level for current devices, reaching an illustrative per-round target of $\\varepsilon_L = 10^{-12}$ for one memory patch requires distance $d \\approx 21$ and 881 physical qubits in this model. This is a per-patch memory figure, not an algorithm budget. A computation uses many patches over many rounds, plus space for routing and magic-state production, so it does not mean that 881 qubits can run $10^{12}$ logical operations. In a separate memory benchmark, [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7) estimate nearly 3,000 physical qubits in total to protect 12 logical qubits with the surface code, compared with 288 for the gross code at $p = 10^{-3}$. That comparison uses the paper's simulated memory performance, not the $10^{-12}$ target used here. Stretching the target by another factor of $10^{-2}$ in logical error rate adds only a few more units of distance, but the qubit count climbs by another few hundred. This overhead motivates the search for codes outside the strict 2D-local family, which is the topic of Part 3.\n"
msgstr "Quadratic ang paglaki ng bilang ng qubit. Ang isang bare distance-$d$ rotated patch ay gumagamit ng $d^2$ data qubit at $d^2-1$ syndrome qubit, kaya $2d^2-1$ physical qubit ang kabuuan sa bawat logical qubit ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7)). Kapag pinagsama ang dalawang scaling, ang pag-abot sa napakababang target na error rate ay nagpapalaki sa $d$ nang logarithmic kaugnay ng target, habang ang physical-qubit footprint ay lumalaki bilang parisukat nito. Ipinapakita nang malinaw ng plot sa ibaba ang trade-off. Sa physical error rate na $p = 10^{-3}$, isang makatwirang antas para sa kasalukuyang mga device, ang pag-abot sa isang illustrative na per-round target na $\\varepsilon_L = 10^{-12}$ para sa isang memory patch ay nangangailangan ng distansyang $d \\approx 21$ at 881 physical qubit sa modelong ito. Ito ay figure para sa memory ng isang patch, hindi budget ng isang algorithm. Ang isang computation ay gumagamit ng maraming patch sa maraming round, kasama pa ang espasyo para sa routing at paggawa ng magic-state, kaya hindi ibig sabihin nito na kayang magpatakbo ng 881 qubit ng $10^{12}$ logical operation. Sa isang hiwalay na memory benchmark, tinatantya ng [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7) na halos 3,000 physical qubit ang kabuuang kailangan para protektahan ang 12 logical qubit gamit ang surface code, kumpara sa 288 para sa gross code sa $p = 10^{-3}$. Ang paghahambing na iyon ay gumagamit ng simulated na memory performance ng paper, hindi ng $10^{-12}$ na target na ginamit dito. Ang pagtaas pa ng target ng karagdagang factor na $10^{-2}$ sa logical error rate ay nagdaragdag lang ng ilang yunit ng distansya, pero ang bilang ng qubit ay tumataas pa ng ilang daan. Ang overhead na ito ang dahilan ng paghahanap ng mga code na labas sa mahigpit na 2D-local na pamilya, na siyang paksa ng Part 3.\n"

