# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.3 Locality, rate, and distance: the Bravyi-Poulin-Terhal bound {#23-locality-rate-and-distance-the-bravyi-poulin-terhal-bound}"
msgstr "2.3 Locality, rate, at distance: ang Bravyi-Poulin-Terhal bound {#23-locality-rate-and-distance-the-bravyi-poulin-terhal-bound}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A useful way to compare codes is by how many logical qubits they pack into a given number of physical qubits. This is the *rate*, written $k/n$, where $k$ is the number of logical qubits encoded and $n$ is the number of physical qubits used. The surface code has rate $k/n = 1/d^2$. Each logical qubit occupies its own $d \\times d$ patch, and the rate vanishes as the distance grows.\n"
msgstr "Isang kapaki-pakinabang na paraan para ihambing ang mga code ay kung ilang logical qubit ang naipapasok nila sa isang tiyak na bilang ng physical qubit. Ito ang *rate*, na isinusulat bilang $k/n$, kung saan ang $k$ ay ang bilang ng naka-encode na logical qubit at ang $n$ ay ang bilang ng ginamit na physical qubit. Ang surface code ay may rate na $k/n = 1/d^2$. Ang bawat logical qubit ay may sariling $d \\times d$ na patch, at ang rate ay nagiging halos zero habang lumalaki ang distansya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This vanishing rate is a property of the geometry, not of the surface code in particular. A result of Bravyi, Poulin, and Terhal ([Bravyi et al., 2010](https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.5200)), the *BPT bound*, says that for any stabilizer code whose checks act only between nearest neighbors on a 2D grid, the product $k\\,d^2$ cannot exceed the number of physical qubits up to a constant. Equivalently, the dimensionless ratio $k\\,d^2/n$ is capped by a constant for every code in this family. The various forms of the surface code (rotated, planar, and toric) all sit near the same $O(1)$ value, with the exact constant depending on how the boundary is handled.\n"
msgstr "Ang pagliit na ito ng rate ay katangian ng geometry, hindi ng surface code lang. Ayon sa resulta nina Bravyi, Poulin, at Terhal ([Bravyi et al., 2010](https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.5200)), ang *BPT bound*, para sa anumang stabilizer code na ang mga check ay kumikilos lang sa pagitan ng pinakamalapit na kapitbahay sa isang 2D grid, ang produktong $k\\,d^2$ ay hindi maaaring lumampas sa bilang ng physical qubit, hanggang sa isang constant. Sa madaling sabi, ang dimensionless na ratio na $k\\,d^2/n$ ay may hangganang constant para sa bawat code sa pamilyang ito. Ang iba't ibang anyo ng surface code (rotated, planar, at toric) ay pare-parehong nasa paligid ng iisang $O(1)$ na halaga, at ang eksaktong constant ay nakadepende sa kung paano hinahawakan ang boundary.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The consequence is a hard trade-off. Within strict 2D locality, increasing the distance to suppress the logical error rate forces the rate to shrink, and the physical-qubit count per logical qubit grows quadratically with distance. Within strict 2D locality, rate and distance cannot both be large, because their product $k\\,d^2$ is capped at $O(n)$ (the BPT bound). Codes that leave the 2D-local family can do better, by an order of magnitude or more. In the table below, $n$ is the code block length used in the code parameters $[[n,k,d]]$. It is not a complete hardware footprint, since syndrome ancillas, routing, couplers, and measurement hardware are not included.\n"
msgstr "Ang resulta ay isang mahigpit na trade-off. Sa loob ng mahigpit na 2D locality, ang pagpapalaki ng distansya para pigilan ang logical error rate ay pumipilit na lumiit ang rate, at ang bilang ng physical qubit bawat logical qubit ay lumalaki nang quadratic kasabay ng distansya. Sa loob ng mahigpit na 2D locality, hindi maaaring pareho nang malaki ang rate at distansya, dahil ang produkto nilang $k\\,d^2$ ay may hangganang $O(n)$ (ang BPT bound). Ang mga code na lumalabas sa 2D-local na pamilya ay mas mahusay, ng isang order of magnitude o higit pa. Sa talahanayan sa ibaba, ang $n$ ay ang block length ng code na ginagamit sa mga parameter ng code na $[[n,k,d]]$. Hindi ito kumpletong hardware footprint, dahil hindi kasama ang mga syndrome ancilla, routing, coupler, at measurement hardware.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| code | family | $[[n, k, d]]$ | $k\\,d^2/n$ | reference |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| rotated surface | 2D-local (surface) | $[[d^2,\\,1,\\,d]]$ | $1$ | [Bombín and Martín-Delgado, 2007](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0703272) |\n"
"| planar surface | 2D-local (surface) | $[[2d^2 - 2d + 1,\\,1,\\,d]]$ | $\\to 1/2$ | [Bravyi and Kitaev, 1998](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9811052) |\n"
"| toric | 2D-local (surface) | $[[2d^2,\\,2,\\,d]]$ | $1$ | [Kitaev, 2003](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9707021) |\n"
