# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.4 The qLDPC landscape {#24-the-qldpc-landscape}"
msgstr "2.4 Ang tanawin ng qLDPC {#24-the-qldpc-landscape}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A *quantum LDPC* (qLDPC) code is a stabilizer code whose *parity-check matrix* is *sparse*. The parity-check matrix is the binary array whose rows are the stabilizer checks (one row per check, one column per qubit, and a 1 wherever a check acts on a qubit). Sparse means that each row and column has only a small, bounded number of 1s, regardless of code size, which is what \"low-density\" means. By this definition, the surface code is itself a qLDPC code, because its weight-4 stabilizers do not grow with $n$. What sets the qLDPC codes of recent interest apart is not that they are sparser than the surface code, but that they relax 2D-locality while keeping sparsity. That relaxation is how they escape the BPT ceiling examined in [Section 2.3](#bpt-bound).\n"
msgstr "Ang *quantum LDPC* (qLDPC) na code ay isang stabilizer code na ang *parity-check matrix* ay *sparse*. Ang parity-check matrix ay ang binary na array na ang mga hanay ay ang mga stabilizer check (isang hanay bawat check, isang kolum bawat qubit, at 1 saanman kumikilos ang check sa isang qubit). Ang sparse ay nangangahulugang ang bawat hanay at kolum ay may maliit at may hangganang bilang lang ng mga 1, anuman ang laki ng code, na siyang kahulugan ng \"low-density\". Ayon sa depinisyong ito, ang surface code mismo ay isang qLDPC code, dahil ang mga weight-4 stabilizer nito ay hindi lumalaki kasabay ng $n$. Ang nagpapaiba sa mga qLDPC code na kinaiinteresan kamakailan ay hindi dahil mas sparse sila kaysa sa surface code, kundi dahil niluluwagan nila ang 2D-locality habang pinananatili ang sparsity. Ganito sila nakakatakas sa BPT ceiling na sinuri sa [Seksyon 2.3](#bpt-bound).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The \"LDPC\" terminology comes from classical coding theory, where low-density parity-check codes have long been known to combine good rates with efficient decoding. The quantum analog inherits the structural advantage of sparse checks while removing the geometric-locality assumption. Several families realize this: *hypergraph-product* codes ([Tillich and Zémor, 2009](https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.0566)), *lifted-product* codes ([Panteleev and Kalachev, 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03654)), *quantum Tanner* codes ([Leverrier and Zémor, 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13641)), and *bivariate-bicycle* codes ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7)). Some of these are *asymptotically good*, keeping constant rate and linear distance as the code grows, which is a property surface codes cannot achieve.\n"
msgstr "Ang terminong \"LDPC\" ay galing sa classical coding theory, kung saan matagal nang kilala na pinagsasama ng low-density parity-check na mga code ang magandang rate at mahusay na decoding. Minamana ng quantum na katumbas ang istruktural na bentahe ng mga sparse na check habang inaalis ang palagay na geometric locality. Ilang pamilya ang gumagawa nito: mga *hypergraph-product* na code ([Tillich and Zémor, 2009](https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.0566)), mga *lifted-product* na code ([Panteleev and Kalachev, 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03654)), mga *quantum Tanner* na code ([Leverrier and Zémor, 2022](https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13641)), at mga *bivariate-bicycle* na code ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7)). Ang ilan sa mga ito ay *asymptotically good*, na nagpapanatili ng constant na rate at linear na distansya habang lumalaki ang code, isang katangiang hindi kayang abutin ng mga surface code.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For this lesson the concrete case study is the bivariate-bicycle family, examined in detail in Part 3. The reason for that choice is not a mathematical claim that bivariate-bicycle is the strongest qLDPC family. It is an architectural alignment. The published IBM superconducting roadmap selects these codes because their thickness-2 check graph fits the proposed superconducting hardware, within the modular architecture discussed in Part 5. Within that scope, the headline result is that, *in the reported circuit-level memory simulation*, bivariate-bicycle codes can match surface-code suppression with about an order of magnitude fewer physical qubits per logical qubit at $p = 10^{-3}$ ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7); see also the IBM [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc)). The cost does not disappear. It moves from physical-qubit count into *connectivity* (the check graph splits into two planar subgraphs and includes long-range edges), *decoding* (minimum-weight matching no longer applies, and qLDPC decoding is an active research area), and *architecture* (modular hardware to support both). Part 3 turns this qLDPC idea into a concrete BB/gross-code case study.\n"
"![Planar qubit grid with long-range connections (top) and the same connectivity on a 3D torus for the gross code (bottom)](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/gross-code-torus.avif)\n"
msgstr ""
"Para sa araling ito, ang konkretong case study ay ang bivariate-bicycle na pamilya, na sinusuri nang detalyado sa Part 3. Ang dahilan ng pagpiling iyon ay hindi isang matematikal na pahayag na ang bivariate-bicycle ang pinakamalakas na qLDPC na pamilya. Ito ay architectural na pagkakahanay. Pinipili ng nailathalang IBM superconducting roadmap ang mga code na ito dahil ang kanilang thickness-2 na check graph ay akma sa iminungkahing superconducting hardware, sa loob ng modular architecture na tinalakay sa Part 5. Sa saklaw na iyon, ang pangunahing resulta ay na, *sa iniulat na circuit-level memory simulation*, kayang tapatan ng mga bivariate-bicycle na code ang suppression ng surface code gamit ang humigit-kumulang isang order of magnitude na mas kaunting physical qubit bawat logical qubit sa $p = 10^{-3}$ ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7); tingnan din ang IBM [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc)). Hindi nawawala ang gastos. Lumilipat ito mula sa bilang ng physical qubit patungo sa *connectivity* (ang check graph ay nahahati sa dalawang planar na subgraph at may mga long-range na edge), *decoding* (hindi na naaangkop ang minimum-weight matching, at ang qLDPC decoding ay aktibong larangan ng pananaliksik), at *architecture* (modular na hardware para suportahan ang pareho). Ginagawa ng Part 3 na konkretong BB/gross-code na case study ang ideyang qLDPC na ito.\n"
"![Planar na grid ng qubit na may long-range na koneksyon (itaas) at ang parehong connectivity sa isang 3D torus para sa gross code (ibaba)](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/gross-code-torus.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Visualization of the gross code $[[144,\\,12,\\,12]]$: 12 logical qubits encoded across 144 data qubits, with 144 syndrome qubits. Realizing the code on a planar quantum chip requires long-range connections that follow the symmetry of a 3D torus. These are the non-local checks that escape the BPT bound. Source: [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc).*\n"
msgstr "*Visualization ng gross code na $[[144,\\,12,\\,12]]$: 12 logical qubit na naka-encode sa 144 data qubit, na may 144 syndrome qubit. Ang pagsasakatuparan ng code sa isang planar na quantum chip ay nangangailangan ng mga long-range na koneksyon na sumusunod sa simetriya ng isang 3D torus. Ito ang mga non-local na check na tumatakas sa BPT bound. Pinagmulan: [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc).*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-1}"
msgstr "Suriin ang pagkaunawa mo {#check-your-understanding-1}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why does the surface code map so naturally onto 2D nearest-neighbor hardware?\n"
msgstr "Bakit natural na tumutugma ang surface code sa 2D nearest-neighbor na hardware?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Its checks are *local*. Each acts on only a few neighboring qubits, so the code lays directly onto a planar grid with nearest-neighbor couplers. That convenience is what the BPT ceiling charges for in rate, and what qLDPC codes trade away.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga check nito ay *local*. Bawat isa ay kumikilos lang sa ilang kalapit na qubit, kaya direktang nailalatag ang code sa planar na grid na may nearest-neighbor na mga coupler. Ang kaginhawahang iyon ang siyang sinisingil ng BPT ceiling sa anyo ng rate, at siyang ipinagpapalit ng mga qLDPC code.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "BB/gross codes can match surface-code suppression with about $10\\times$ fewer physical qubits for the memory task, in the reported circuit-level simulation. Where does the \"saved\" cost reappear?\n"
msgstr "Kayang tapatan ng mga BB/gross code ang suppression ng surface code gamit ang humigit-kumulang $10\\times$ na mas kaunting physical qubit para sa memory task, sa iniulat na circuit-level simulation. Saan muling lumilitaw ang \"natipid\" na gastos?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "It does not vanish. It *moves*. The weight-6 non-local checks demand long-range connectivity, implemented with c-couplers within a chip in the IBM roadmap. Scaling the modular architecture also calls for inter-module l-couplers ([Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)). Decoding uses methods such as belief propagation with ordered-statistics decoding (BP+OSD) or Relay-BP rather than surface-code matching ([Section 5.2](#decoder-families)). (This does *not* mean that qLDPC is strictly better, as [Section 3.5](#case-study-limits) explains, and the noise threshold and syndrome depth stay comparable to the surface code.)\n"
msgstr "Hindi ito nawawala. *Lumilipat* ito. Ang mga weight-6 na non-local na check ay nangangailangan ng long-range na connectivity, na ipinapatupad gamit ang mga c-coupler sa loob ng isang chip sa IBM roadmap. Ang pag-scale ng modular architecture ay nangangailangan din ng mga inter-module na l-coupler ([Seksyon 5.3](#modular-architecture)). Ang decoding ay gumagamit ng mga pamamaraan tulad ng belief propagation na may ordered-statistics decoding (BP+OSD) o Relay-BP sa halip na surface-code matching ([Seksyon 5.2](#decoder-families)). (Hindi *ito* nangangahulugan na mas mahusay ang qLDPC sa lahat ng aspeto, tulad ng ipinaliwanag sa [Seksyon 3.5](#case-study-limits), at ang noise threshold at syndrome depth ay nananatiling katulad ng sa surface code.)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 3: BB/gross codes as a qLDPC case study {#part-3-bbgross-codes-as-a-qldpc-case-study}"
