# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "3.2 Memory: the gross code $[[144,12,12]]$ (Bravyi et al., 2024) {#32-memory-the-gross-code-1441212-bravyi-et-al-2024}"
msgstr "3.2 Memory: ang gross code na $[[144,12,12]]$ (Bravyi et al., 2024) {#32-memory-the-gross-code-1441212-bravyi-et-al-2024}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Section 2.2](#surface-overhead) showed that surface-code memory is expensive at deep logical-error targets, and storing many logical qubits compounds the cost. Each logical qubit needs its own $d \\times d$ patch, and the per-logical budget grows quadratically with distance. The BPT bound ([Section 2.3](#bpt-bound)) explains structurally why this overhead is hard to reduce within strict 2D locality. The natural question is whether stepping outside that constraint can lower the memory budget at concretely useful distances, while keeping the threshold and decoder properties that made surface codes the standard baseline.\n"
msgstr "Ipinakita ng [Seksyon 2.2](#surface-overhead) na magastos ang surface-code memory sa malalalim na logical-error na target, at ang pag-iimbak ng maraming logical qubit ay nagpapalala sa gastos. Ang bawat logical qubit ay nangangailangan ng sariling $d \\times d$ na patch, at ang budget bawat logical ay lumalaki nang quadratic kasabay ng distansya. Ipinapaliwanag ng BPT bound ([Seksyon 2.3](#bpt-bound)) nang istruktural kung bakit mahirap bawasan ang overhead na ito sa loob ng mahigpit na 2D locality. Ang natural na tanong ay kung kayang pababain ng paglabas sa limitasyong iyon ang memory budget sa mga konkretong kapaki-pakinabang na distansya, habang pinananatili ang mga katangian ng threshold at decoder na naging dahilan kaya ang mga surface code ang karaniwang baseline.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The paper by [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7) introduces the bivariate-bicycle (BB) family, develops the construction worked through in [Section 3.1](#bb-construction), and proposes specific code instances for evaluation. The headline candidate is the *gross code* $[[144, 12, 12]]$. One block holds 12 logical qubits in 144 data qubits plus 144 syndrome ancillas, for 288 physical qubits in total. Its Tanner graph makes the structure concrete. Every vertex has six incident edges, four short-range (north, south, east, west) and two long-range, with the data qubits split across two registers $q(L)$ and $q(R)$.\n"
msgstr "Ipinakilala ng paper nina [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7) ang bivariate-bicycle (BB) na pamilya, binuo ang construction na tinalakay sa [Seksyon 3.1](#bb-construction), at nagmungkahi ng mga tiyak na instance ng code para sa pagsusuri. Ang pangunahing kandidato ay ang *gross code* na $[[144, 12, 12]]$. Ang isang block ay naglalaman ng 12 logical qubit sa 144 data qubit kasama ang 144 syndrome ancilla, kaya 288 physical qubit ang kabuuan. Ang Tanner graph nito ay ginagawang konkreto ang istruktura. Ang bawat vertex ay may anim na incident na edge, apat na short-range (hilaga, timog, silangan, kanluran) at dalawang long-range, kung saan ang mga data qubit ay nahahati sa dalawang register na $q(L)$ at $q(R)$.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Tanner graphs of the surface code (a) and the gross BB code [[144,12,12]] embedded into a torus (b), with four short-range and two long-range edges per vertex](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/tanner-graphs.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Tanner graphs of the surface code (a) and the gross BB code [[144,12,12]] embedded into a torus (b), with four short-range and two long-range edges per vertex](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/tanner-graphs.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Tanner graphs of (a) the surface code and (b) the gross BB code $[[144, 12, 12]]$ embedded into a torus. Each data/check vertex has six incident edges, four short-range plus two long-range (only a few long-range edges are drawn, to avoid clutter). Blue and orange circles are the $q(L)$ and $q(R)$ data registers. Source: [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7), Nature 627, 778. Figure from the IBM Quantum blog, [Nature paper announcement](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/nature-qldpc-error-correction).*\n"
msgstr "*Mga Tanner graph ng (a) surface code at (b) ng gross BB code na $[[144, 12, 12]]$ na naka-embed sa isang torus. Ang bawat data/check na vertex ay may anim na incident na edge, apat na short-range at dalawang long-range (ilang long-range na edge lang ang iginuhit, para iwasan ang kalat). Ang asul at kahel na bilog ay ang mga data register na $q(L)$ at $q(R)$. Pinagmulan: [Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7), Nature 627, 778. Figure mula sa IBM Quantum blog, [anunsyo ng Nature paper](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/nature-qldpc-error-correction).*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The full simulation evaluates a short, constant-depth syndrome-measurement cycle (seven layers of CNOT gates) on that non-local connectivity graph, under a *circuit-level* noise model, with stochastic Pauli faults applied at every gate and measurement of the syndrome circuit, including the ancillas and the operations themselves, not just at idle data qubits. (This is the realistic model for hardware estimation. *Code-capacity* models that fault only the data are abstract and less predictive of hardware performance.) The specific parameters here use standard superconducting-style depolarizing noise strengths (under *depolarizing* noise each qubit independently suffers a random $X$, $Y$, or $Z$ error with equal probability). The published paper compares the gross code with surface-code memories storing the same 12 logical qubits in independent rotated patches. Each patch uses $2d^2-1$ physical qubits, including data and syndrome qubits, so the distance-11 reference uses 241 qubits per patch.\n"
msgstr "Sinusuri ng buong simulation ang isang maikli at constant-depth na syndrome-measurement cycle (pitong layer ng mga CNOT gate) sa non-local na connectivity graph na iyon, sa ilalim ng *circuit-level* na noise model, na may stochastic na mga Pauli fault na inilalapat sa bawat gate at measurement ng syndrome circuit, kasama ang mga ancilla at ang mga operasyon mismo, hindi lang sa mga idle na data qubit. (Ito ang makatotohanang modelo para sa pagtantya ng hardware. Ang mga *code-capacity* na modelo na nagpapa-fault lang sa data ay abstract at mas mahina sa paghula ng performance ng hardware.) Ang mga tiyak na parameter dito ay gumagamit ng karaniwang superconducting-style na lakas ng depolarizing noise (sa ilalim ng *depolarizing* noise, ang bawat qubit ay nang-isa-isang nagkakaroon ng random na $X$, $Y$, o $Z$ error na may pantay na probabilidad). Inihahambing ng nailathalang paper ang gross code sa mga surface-code memory na nag-iimbak ng parehong 12 logical qubit sa mga independent na rotated patch. Ang bawat patch ay gumagamit ng $2d^2-1$ physical qubit, kasama ang data at syndrome qubit, kaya ang distance-11 na reference ay gumagamit ng 241 qubit bawat patch.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Under that circuit-level model, the gross code preserves all 12 logical qubits for on the order of a million error-correction cycles at $p = 10^{-3}$, using 288 physical qubits in total ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7); the IBM [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc) blog). The paper also reports a *pseudo-threshold* of about 0.7% under the same circuit-level noise model (the abstract calls it the error threshold), comparable to the surface-code threshold and a precondition for hardware viability. (A *threshold* is the asymptotic physical-error rate below which increasing code distance reduces logical error. A *pseudo-threshold* is the empirical crossover measured for specific finite distances under a specific noise model. In this paper, it is the break-even error rate at which the logical error rate $p_L$ of the code block equals $kp$, the approximate probability that at least one of $k$ unencoded qubits suffers an error. The two are typically close but not identical.) The paper reports roughly an order-of-magnitude reduction in physical-qubit count for the memory benchmark, although the compared codes have different logical-error curves. The same paper presents a small family of related BB codes ($[[72,12,6]]$, $[[90,8,10]]$, $[[108,8,10]]$, $[[144,12,12]]$, $[[288,12,18]]$, and $[[360,12, \\le 24]]$), characterizing how the rate, distance, and required hardware budget trade off as the polynomials and lattice dimensions are varied (Bravyi et al. 2024, Table 1 and Extended Data Table 1). The headline comparison is easiest to see in a table:\n"
msgstr "Sa ilalim ng circuit-level na modelong iyon, napapanatili ng gross code ang lahat ng 12 logical qubit sa loob ng humigit-kumulang isang milyong error-correction cycle sa $p = 10^{-3}$, gamit ang 288 physical qubit sa kabuuan ([Bravyi et al., 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07107-7); ang IBM [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc) blog). Iniuulat din ng paper ang *pseudo-threshold* na humigit-kumulang 0.7% sa ilalim ng parehong circuit-level noise model (tinatawag ito ng abstract na error threshold), na maihahambing sa threshold ng surface code at isang paunang kondisyon para maging posible ang hardware. (Ang *threshold* ay ang asymptotic na physical-error rate kung saan ang pagtaas ng distansya ng code ay nagpapababa ng logical error. Ang *pseudo-threshold* ay ang empirikal na crossover na nasukat para sa mga tiyak na may hangganang distansya sa ilalim ng isang tiyak na noise model. Sa paper na ito, ito ang break-even na error rate kung saan ang logical error rate na $p_L$ ng code block ay katumbas ng $kp$, ang tinatayang probabilidad na kahit isa sa $k$ na unencoded na qubit ay magkaroon ng error. Karaniwang magkalapit ang dalawa pero hindi magkapareho.) Iniuulat ng paper ang humigit-kumulang isang order-of-magnitude na pagbawas sa bilang ng physical qubit para sa memory benchmark, bagaman ang mga inihambing na code ay may magkaibang logical-error na kurba. Ang parehong paper ay nagpapakita ng maliit na pamilya ng magkakaugnay na BB code ($[[72,12,6]]$, $[[90,8,10]]$, $[[108,8,10]]$, $[[144,12,12]]$, $[[288,12,18]]$, at $[[360,12, \\le 24]]$), na naglalarawan kung paano nagtatrade-off ang rate, distansya, at kinakailangang hardware budget habang binabago ang mga polynomial at dimensyon ng lattice (Bravyi et al. 2024, Table 1 at Extended Data Table 1). Pinakamadaling makita ang pangunahing paghahambing sa isang talahanayan:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Memory for 12 logical qubits ($p = 10^{-3}$) | Physical qubits | versus gross | Source |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| Gross code $[[144, 12, 12]]$ | 288 (144 data + 144 ancilla) | 1$\\times$ | Bravyi et al. 2024 |\n"
"| Surface code (12 patches, $d = 11$) | 2,892 ($12 \\times 241$) | ~10$\\times$ | Distance-11 reference in Bravyi et al. 2024 |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Memory para sa 12 logical qubit ($p = 10^{-3}$) | Physical qubit | kumpara sa gross | Pinagmulan |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| Gross code $[[144, 12, 12]]$ | 288 (144 data + 144 ancilla) | 1$\\times$ | Bravyi et al. 2024 |\n"
"| Surface code (12 patch, $d = 11$) | 2,892 ($12 \\times 241$) | ~10$\\times$ | Distance-11 na reference sa Bravyi et al. 2024 |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The table compares physical-qubit counts using the paper's distance-11 surface-code reference. It does not assert equal logical-error rates at that distance; the performance comparison is given by the paper's circuit-level simulations.\n"
"This result establishes that for the *memory* task (preserving encoded logical states across repeated error-correction cycles, with no logical gates yet performed), qLDPC codes with weight-6 checks on a 2D-superconducting layout can match the memory target in the reported simulation at roughly an order of magnitude smaller physical-qubit footprint. The demonstration is based on realistic circuit-level simulation rather than abstract code-capacity bounds, which is the basis on which surface-code overhead numbers are also reported. The reported pseudo-threshold of about 0.7% (0.8% in the earlier preprint), on par with the surface code, indicates the gain is not bought by sacrificing tolerance for physical noise.\n"
msgstr ""
"Inihahambing ng talahanayan ang bilang ng physical qubit gamit ang distance-11 na surface-code reference ng paper. Hindi nito sinasabing pantay ang logical-error rate sa distansyang iyon; ang paghahambing ng performance ay ibinibigay ng mga circuit-level simulation ng paper.\n"
"Pinatutunayan ng resultang ito na para sa *memory* task (pagpapanatili ng mga naka-encode na logical state sa paulit-ulit na error-correction cycle, na wala pang logical gate na isinasagawa), kayang abutin ng mga qLDPC code na may weight-6 na check sa isang 2D-superconducting na layout ang memory target sa iniulat na simulation sa humigit-kumulang isang order of magnitude na mas maliit na physical-qubit footprint. Ang demonstrasyon ay batay sa makatotohanang circuit-level simulation sa halip na abstract na code-capacity bound, na siya ring batayan ng pag-uulat ng mga overhead number ng surface code. Ang iniulat na pseudo-threshold na humigit-kumulang 0.7% (0.8% sa naunang preprint), na kapantay ng surface code, ay nagpapahiwatig na ang pakinabang ay hindi nakuha sa pagsasakripisyo ng tolerance sa physical noise.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"What it does not establish is the corresponding result for *computation* on the encoded qubits, which requires logical Clifford and non-Clifford operations that the memory protocol does not exercise. Those are the subject of [Section 3.4](#computation). It also does not yet establish hardware feasibility. The simulations assume the seven-layer syndrome cycle on a connectivity graph that current commercial superconducting chips do not implement. This motivates the chip-level architecture program (c-couplers, biplanar layout, and the Loon processor introduced in [Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) that is needed to turn the simulated code into a buildable design. The remaining subsections of Part 3 work through these missing pieces (scaling in [3.3](#two-gross) and computation in [3.4](#computation)), and decoding and modular hardware are discussed in Part 5.\n"
"<span id=\"two-gross\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang hindi nito napapatunayan ay ang katumbas na resulta para sa *computation* sa mga naka-encode na qubit, na nangangailangan ng logical Clifford at non-Clifford na mga operasyon na hindi ginagamit ng memory protocol. Ang mga iyon ang paksa ng [Section 3.4](#computation). Hindi pa rin nito napapatunayan ang hardware feasibility. Ipinapalagay ng mga simulation ang pitong-layer na syndrome cycle sa isang connectivity graph na hindi pa ipinapatupad ng kasalukuyang komersyal na superconducting chip. Ito ang dahilan ng chip-level architecture program (c-couplers, biplanar layout, at ang Loon processor na ipinakilala sa [Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) na kailangan para gawing buildable na disenyo ang na-simulate na code. Tatalakayin ng natitirang mga subsection ng Part 3 ang mga kulang na piraso (scaling sa [3.3](#two-gross) at computation sa [3.4](#computation)), at ang decoding at modular hardware ay tatalakayin sa Part 5.\n"
"<span id=\"two-gross\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "3.3 Scaling the case study: two-gross {#33-scaling-the-case-study-two-gross}"
msgstr "3.3 Pag-scale ng case study: two-gross {#33-scaling-the-case-study-two-gross}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The gross code is one entry in the BB family, not a single endpoint. The natural next question is whether the same construction extends to higher distance without changing the broader architecture, and what the qubit cost of that extension looks like. The paper's answer is the *two-gross code* $[[288,12,18]]$, obtained from a related polynomial choice on a larger lattice within the same bivariate-bicycle framework. The code keeps the same 12 logical qubits, raises the distance from 12 to 18, and doubles the data-qubit count from 144 to 288 (with the total physical-qubit count rising from 288 to 576 once syndrome ancillas are included).\n"
msgstr "Ang gross code ay isa lamang entry sa BB family, hindi nag-iisang dulo. Ang natural na susunod na tanong ay kung maaari bang palawakin ang parehong konstruksyon patungo sa mas mataas na distance nang hindi binabago ang mas malawak na architecture, at magkano ang magiging gastos sa qubit ng extension na iyon. Ang sagot ng paper ay ang *two-gross code* $[[288,12,18]]$, na nakukuha mula sa kaugnay na polynomial choice sa mas malaking lattice sa loob ng parehong bivariate-bicycle framework. Pinapanatili ng code ang parehong 12 logical qubit, itinataas ang distance mula 12 hanggang 18, at dinodoble ang bilang ng data-qubit mula 144 hanggang 288 (kung saan tumataas ang kabuuang bilang ng physical qubit mula 288 hanggang 576 kapag isinama ang mga syndrome ancilla).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Consider the BPT-ratio entries from [Section 2.3](#bpt-bound). Gross sits at $k\\,d^2 / n = 12$ and two-gross sits at $12 \\cdot 18^2 / 288 = 13.5$. These ratios illustrate the benefit of relaxing strict 2D locality; the BPT bound does not apply to these non-local checks. Gross and two-gross are two finite-distance operating points. The former uses fewer qubits, while the latter spends a doubled data-qubit budget to reach a higher distance. The progression from gross to two-gross increases the qubit and connectivity requirements. The IBM modular architecture ([Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) is designed to connect memory, processing, and other modules as the system scales.\n"
msgstr "Tingnan ang mga BPT-ratio entry mula sa [Section 2.3](#bpt-bound). Ang Gross ay nasa $k\\,d^2 / n = 12$ at ang two-gross ay nasa $12 \\cdot 18^2 / 288 = 13.5$. Ipinapakita ng mga ratio na ito ang benepisyo ng pagluwag sa mahigpit na 2D locality; hindi nalalapat ang BPT bound sa mga non-local na check na ito. Ang Gross at two-gross ay dalawang finite-distance na operating point. Ang una ay gumagamit ng mas kaunting qubit, habang ang huli ay gumagastos ng dalawang beses na data-qubit budget para maabot ang mas mataas na distance. Ang pag-usad mula gross patungong two-gross ay nagpapataas ng mga kinakailangan sa qubit at connectivity. Ang IBM modular architecture ([Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) ay dinisenyo para ikonekta ang memory, processing, at iba pang module habang lumalaki ang sistema.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This scaling step establishes that the BB family extends by *changing the polynomial choice on a larger lattice*, not by replicating independent code blocks (as a surface-code memory does when growing across multiple logical qubits). The same algebraic framework, the same syndrome-extraction circuit shape, and the same decoding problem class apply across the family. These finite-size examples do not establish *asymptotic goodness*, which requires a nonzero limiting rate and distance growing linearly with code length. The case-study point is that within the *finite-distance regime* relevant to near-term fault-tolerance roadmaps, the BB family offers concrete operating points whose costs can be evaluated together with their decoder and connectivity requirements.\n"
"<span id=\"computation\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Pinapatunayan ng hakbang na ito sa pag-scale na lumalawak ang BB family sa pamamagitan ng *pagpapalit ng polynomial choice sa mas malaking lattice*, hindi sa pag-uulit ng mga independent na code block (tulad ng ginagawa ng surface-code memory kapag lumalaki sa maraming logical qubit). Ang parehong algebraic framework, ang parehong hugis ng syndrome-extraction circuit, at ang parehong klase ng decoding problem ay nalalapat sa buong family. Hindi pinapatunayan ng mga finite-size na halimbawang ito ang *asymptotic goodness*, na nangangailangan ng nonzero na limiting rate at distance na lumalaki nang linear kasabay ng haba ng code. Ang punto ng case study ay sa loob ng *finite-distance regime* na mahalaga sa mga near-term na fault-tolerance roadmap, nag-aalok ang BB family ng mga konkretong operating point na ang gastos ay maaaring suriin kasama ng kanilang mga requirement sa decoder at connectivity.\n"
"<span id=\"computation\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "3.4 What computation adds beyond memory {#34-what-computation-adds-beyond-memory}"
msgstr "3.4 Ang idinaragdag ng computation bukod sa memory {#34-what-computation-adds-beyond-memory}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The memory result of [Section 3.2](#gross-memory) protects encoded logical states, but it does not perform operations on them. A useful computation requires logical *gates* (at minimum, a universal set composed of Clifford and non-Clifford operations) and a way to apply them between code blocks. The BB family carries an additional structural challenge here. Because checks span the chip non-locally, even simple operations within a single BB block require care to preserve the code's properties, and operations *between* BB blocks require architectural pieces (couplers, adapters, and magic-state factories) that are not part of the memory construction.\n"
msgstr "Pinoprotektahan ng memory result sa [Section 3.2](#gross-memory) ang mga naka-encode na logical state, pero hindi ito nagsasagawa ng mga operasyon sa mga ito. Ang kapaki-pakinabang na computation ay nangangailangan ng logical *gate* (kahit man lang isang universal set na binubuo ng Clifford at non-Clifford na mga operasyon) at paraan para ilapat ang mga ito sa pagitan ng mga code block. May dagdag na structural na hamon ang BB family dito. Dahil sumasaklaw ang mga check sa chip nang non-local, kahit ang simpleng mga operasyon sa loob ng iisang BB block ay nangangailangan ng pag-iingat para mapanatili ang mga katangian ng code, at ang mga operasyon *sa pagitan* ng mga BB block ay nangangailangan ng mga architectural na piraso (couplers, adapters, at magic-state factories) na hindi bahagi ng memory construction.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As a case study, we now look at *Tour de gross* ([Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094)), which introduces the *bicycle architecture*: a fault-tolerant *system* composed of independently fabricated modules. Each code module contains a memory code block and an attached *logical processing unit* (LPU) implementing logical operations through generalized lattice surgery. Separate magic-state factory modules supply non-Clifford resources, and *universal adapters* implement inter-module logical operations. The same paper evaluates the architecture against six design criteria: *fault-tolerant* (logical errors are suppressed sufficiently for the intended algorithms to succeed), *addressable* (individual logical qubits can be prepared or measured during the computation), *universal* (the full logical gate set, Clifford plus a non-Clifford gate, is available), *adaptive*, *modular* (built from independently fabricated, interchangeable modules), and *efficient* (low physical-qubit and time overhead per logical operation). In the paper's terms, *adaptive* means that syndrome measurements are decoded in real time and can change the quantum instructions that follow, which is genuine real-time feedforward. (Throughout this lesson, we instead treat corrections as an *offline frame update*. This is a deliberate simulation and teaching convenience that maintains short and exact code cells. Real fault-tolerant computation requires the adaptive, real-time feedforward capability described previously. The $S$ correction after $T$ injection and the lattice-surgery byproducts in Part 4 are the points where later gates depend on earlier measurement outcomes, so they cannot all be deferred offline.) The paper reports end-to-end resource estimates suggesting that order-of-magnitude larger logical circuits can be executed at a given physical-qubit budget than under surface-code architectures.\n"
"![Modular fault-tolerant architecture for BB codes: code block, LPU, adapter, T-factory](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/bicycle-architecture.avif)\n"
msgstr ""
"Bilang case study, tingnan natin ngayon ang *Tour de gross* ([Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094)), na nagpapakilala ng *bicycle architecture*: isang fault-tolerant na *sistema* na binubuo ng mga module na hiwa-hiwalay na ginawa. Ang bawat code module ay may memory code block at nakakabit na *logical processing unit* (LPU) na nagpapatupad ng mga logical na operasyon sa pamamagitan ng generalized lattice surgery. Ang hiwalay na mga magic-state factory module ang nagsusuplay ng mga non-Clifford na resource, at ang *universal adapters* ang nagpapatupad ng mga inter-module na logical na operasyon. Sinusuri ng parehong paper ang architecture batay sa anim na design criteria: *fault-tolerant* (sapat na napipigilan ang mga logical error para magtagumpay ang mga nilalayong algorithm), *addressable* (maaaring ihanda o sukatin ang mga indibidwal na logical qubit habang nagko-compute), *universal* (available ang buong logical gate set, Clifford at isang non-Clifford gate), *adaptive*, *modular* (binuo mula sa mga module na hiwa-hiwalay na ginawa at mapagpapalit), at *efficient* (mababang physical-qubit at time overhead bawat logical na operasyon). Sa mga termino ng paper, ang *adaptive* ay nangangahulugang ang mga syndrome measurement ay dine-decode sa real time at maaaring baguhin ang mga quantum instruction na susunod, na tunay na real-time feedforward. (Sa buong lesson na ito, itinuturing natin ang mga correction bilang *offline frame update*. Ito ay sadyang kaginhawaan sa simulation at pagtuturo na nagpapanatiling maikli at eksakto ng mga code cell. Ang tunay na fault-tolerant computation ay nangangailangan ng adaptive at real-time na feedforward capability na inilarawan kanina. Ang $S$ correction pagkatapos ng $T$ injection at ang mga lattice-surgery byproduct sa Part 4 ang mga punto kung saan umaasa ang mga susunod na gate sa mga naunang resulta ng measurement, kaya hindi lahat ay maaaring ipagpaliban nang offline.) Iniuulat ng paper ang mga end-to-end na resource estimate na nagmumungkahing mas malalaking logical circuit, sa antas ng order of magnitude, ang maaaring maisagawa sa isang takdang physical-qubit budget kumpara sa mga surface-code architecture.\n"
"![Modular na fault-tolerant architecture para sa mga BB code: code block, LPU, adapter, T-factory](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/bicycle-architecture.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*The bicycle architecture: a fault-tolerant modular system based on bivariate-bicycle codes. Each code module combines a memory code block with a logical processing unit (LPU) for logical operations through generalized lattice surgery. Separate magic-state factory modules supply non-Clifford resources, and universal adapters connect modules. Source: [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc); architectural details in [Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094).*\n"
"The key question is whether the memory-level advantage from [Section 3.2](#gross-memory) survives the additional overhead of computation, including logical gates, magic-state resources, inter-module routing, decoding, and hardware layout. The case-study argument in Yoder et al. 2025 is that a BB-based architecture can remain competitive with surface-code-based architectures after these overheads are included, preserving part of the memory advantage. The precise margin at the full-algorithm level is still an active resource-estimation question, and the supporting evidence is architectural analysis rather than an experimental demonstration. Part 4 of this lesson works through the universal-gate side of fault-tolerant computation, including Clifford structure, non-Clifford resources, and magic-state distillation. It also covers the *single-block distillation* proposal of [Xu et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20546), which runs a magic-state factory *inside a single BB code block*. That proposal reuses the block's own logical capacity through *qubit recycling* (repurposing the same physical qubits for successive distillation steps) rather than dedicating a separate code block.\n"
"<span id=\"case-study-limits\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"*Ang bicycle architecture: isang fault-tolerant na modular na sistema batay sa mga bivariate-bicycle code. Pinagsasama ng bawat code module ang memory code block at logical processing unit (LPU) para sa mga logical na operasyon sa pamamagitan ng generalized lattice surgery. Ang hiwalay na mga magic-state factory module ang nagsusuplay ng mga non-Clifford na resource, at ikinokonekta ng mga universal adapter ang mga module. Source: [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc); mga detalye ng architecture sa [Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094).*\n"
"Ang mahalagang tanong ay kung nananatili ba ang memory-level na bentahe mula sa [Section 3.2](#gross-memory) sa kabila ng karagdagang overhead ng computation, kasama ang mga logical gate, magic-state resource, inter-module routing, decoding, at hardware layout. Ang argumento ng case study sa Yoder et al. 2025 ay maaaring manatiling kompetitibo ang isang BB-based na architecture sa mga surface-code-based na architecture kahit isama ang mga overhead na ito, na nagpapanatili ng bahagi ng bentahe sa memory. Ang eksaktong margin sa antas ng buong algorithm ay aktibo pa ring tanong sa resource estimation, at ang sumusuportang ebidensya ay architectural analysis sa halip na eksperimental na demonstrasyon. Tinatalakay ng Part 4 ng lesson na ito ang universal-gate na bahagi ng fault-tolerant computation, kasama ang Clifford structure, mga non-Clifford na resource, at magic-state distillation. Saklaw din nito ang *single-block distillation* na panukala ng [Xu et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20546), na nagpapatakbo ng magic-state factory *sa loob ng isang BB code block*. Ginagamit muli ng panukalang iyon ang sariling logical capacity ng block sa pamamagitan ng *qubit recycling* (muling paggamit ng parehong physical qubit para sa magkakasunod na distillation step) sa halip na maglaan ng hiwalay na code block.\n"
"<span id=\"case-study-limits\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "3.5 What this study shows and what it does not prove {#35-what-this-study-shows-and-what-it-does-not-prove}"
msgstr "3.5 Ang ipinapakita at hindi napapatunayan ng pag-aaral na ito {#35-what-this-study-shows-and-what-it-does-not-prove}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Across Sections 3.1 through 3.4, we walked through the BB construction ([3.1](#bb-construction)), the memory benchmark that placed the gross code on the IBM roadmap ([3.2](#gross-memory)), the family's scaling behavior to higher distances ([3.3](#two-gross)), and what architectural pieces computation adds beyond memory ([3.4](#computation)). What the case study collectively *shows* is a coherent multi-paper trajectory: a fully specified qLDPC code family, a published memory accounting at realistic circuit-level noise, a demonstrated scaling step to higher distance within the same construction, and a system-level architecture proposal that addresses universal computation on the encoded qubits.\n"
msgstr "Sa Sections 3.1 hanggang 3.4, tinalakay natin ang BB construction ([3.1](#bb-construction)), ang memory benchmark na naglagay sa gross code sa IBM roadmap ([3.2](#gross-memory)), ang scaling behavior ng family patungo sa mas mataas na distance ([3.3](#two-gross)), at kung anong mga architectural na piraso ang idinaragdag ng computation bukod sa memory ([3.4](#computation)). Ang kolektibong *ipinapakita* ng case study ay isang magkakaugnay na trajectory mula sa maraming paper: isang ganap na natukoy na qLDPC code family, isang nailathalang memory accounting sa makatotohanang circuit-level noise, isang naipakitang scaling step patungo sa mas mataas na distance sa loob ng parehong construction, at isang system-level na architecture proposal na tumutugon sa universal computation sa mga naka-encode na qubit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "What the study does *not* prove:\n"
msgstr "Ang *hindi* napapatunayan ng pag-aaral:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Optimality.** Bivariate-bicycle codes are not necessarily the optimal qLDPC choice for any specific algorithm or platform. Hypergraph-product, lifted-product, and quantum Tanner codes exist and have their own asymptotic and finite-size advantages. The BB selection in the IBM roadmap is an *architectural alignment*, because their thickness-2 check graphs suit the proposed superconducting hardware and modular architecture. It is not a mathematical optimality claim.\n"
msgstr "**Optimality.** Ang mga bivariate-bicycle code ay hindi kinakailangang pinakamainam na qLDPC na pagpipilian para sa anumang partikular na algorithm o platform. Umiiral ang mga hypergraph-product, lifted-product, at quantum Tanner code at may sarili silang asymptotic at finite-size na bentahe. Ang pagpili sa BB sa IBM roadmap ay isang *architectural alignment*, dahil angkop ang kanilang thickness-2 na check graph sa iminumungkahing superconducting hardware at modular architecture. Hindi ito mathematical na optimality claim.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Algorithmic resource counts.** The roughly order-of-magnitude *memory* reduction reported by Bravyi et al. is for a specific noise model and target. Real algorithms have additional resource requirements (gates, magic-state distillation, routing, and real-time decoder latency) that the memory accounting does not capture. The most honest summary is *an order-of-magnitude reduction at the memory task, with computational and system-level overheads still being mapped out*.\n"
msgstr "**Algorithmic resource counts.** Ang humigit-kumulang order-of-magnitude na pagbawas sa *memory* na iniulat ni Bravyi et al. ay para sa partikular na noise model at target. Ang mga totoong algorithm ay may karagdagang resource requirement (mga gate, magic-state distillation, routing, at real-time na decoder latency) na hindi saklaw ng memory accounting. Ang pinakatapat na buod ay *order-of-magnitude na pagbawas sa memory task, habang minamapa pa ang mga computational at system-level na overhead*.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Hardware existence.** The connectivity required by the gross code at full scale has not been built at the time of writing. The IBM chip roadmap (the lineage table in [Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) is the proposed path to building it. Whether the timeline holds is a matter of engineering execution rather than of any published code-theory result.\n"
msgstr "**Hardware existence.** Ang connectivity na kailangan ng gross code sa buong sukat ay hindi pa nabubuo sa oras ng pagsulat. Ang IBM chip roadmap (ang lineage table sa [Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) ang iminumungkahing landas para buuin ito. Kung matutupad ang timeline ay usapin ng engineering execution at hindi ng anumang nailathalang resulta sa code theory.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The bivariate-bicycle case study is an instance of how a recent QEC innovation works through the full stack (code, circuit, architecture, decoder, and integration plan) for one specific platform. Part 4 turns to the universal fault-tolerant computation problem in its own right, going deeper into Clifford and non-Clifford structure. Part 5 returns to QEC as a quantum-classical system across decoders, modular hardware, and other platforms, where the *Relay-BP* decoder ([Müller et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01779)) and the IBM modular-chip roadmap re-enter as the system-level companions to this code-level story.\n"
msgstr "Ang bivariate-bicycle case study ay isang halimbawa kung paano pinagdadaanan ng isang kamakailang QEC innovation ang buong stack (code, circuit, architecture, decoder, at integration plan) para sa isang partikular na platform. Tinutugunan ng Part 4 ang problema ng universal fault-tolerant computation sa sarili nitong karapatan, na mas malalim sa Clifford at non-Clifford structure. Bumabalik ang Part 5 sa QEC bilang quantum-classical na sistema sa mga decoder, modular hardware, at iba pang platform, kung saan muling lumilitaw ang *Relay-BP* decoder ([Müller et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01779)) at ang IBM modular-chip roadmap bilang mga system-level na kasama ng kuwentong ito sa antas ng code.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-2}"
msgstr "Suriin ang iyong pag-unawa {#check-your-understanding-2}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bravyi et al. 2024 report that the gross code $[[144,12,12]]$ holds 12 logical qubits for $\\sim\\!10^6$ cycles at $p=10^{-3}$ using 288 physical qubits, about $10\\times$ fewer than equivalent surface-code memory. State precisely what this does, and does not, establish.\n"
msgstr "Iniulat ni Bravyi et al. 2024 na ang gross code $[[144,12,12]]$ ay nag-iimbak ng 12 logical qubit sa loob ng $\\sim\\!10^6$ cycle sa $p=10^{-3}$ gamit ang 288 physical qubit, humigit-kumulang $10\\times$ na mas kaunti kaysa sa katumbas na surface-code memory. Sabihin nang eksakto kung ano ang napapatunayan at hindi napapatunayan nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "It establishes a *memory* advantage, validated by circuit-level *simulation*. It does **not** show the advantage survives full computation (logical gates, magic-state distillation, and decoder latency all add cost, as discussed from [Section 3.4](#computation) onward), it is **not** an experimental hardware demonstration, and it does **not** make the surface code obsolete (it is still the baseline). [Section 3.4](#computation) (Tour de gross) argues the advantage *might* carry into computation, but that is an architectural analysis, not yet an experiment.\n"
msgstr "Pinapatunayan nito ang isang *memory* na bentahe, na na-validate sa pamamagitan ng circuit-level na *simulation*. **Hindi** nito ipinapakita na nananatili ang bentahe sa buong computation (ang mga logical gate, magic-state distillation, at decoder latency ay pawang nagdaragdag ng gastos, gaya ng tinalakay mula [Section 3.4](#computation) pasulong), **hindi** ito eksperimental na hardware demonstration, at **hindi** nito ginagawang lipas na ang surface code (ito pa rin ang baseline). Ipinapaliwanag ng [Section 3.4](#computation) (Tour de gross) na *maaaring* madala ang bentahe sa computation, pero isa itong architectural analysis, hindi pa eksperimento.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 4: Universal fault-tolerant computation {#part-4-universal-fault-tolerant-computation}"
msgstr "Part 4: Universal na fault-tolerant computation {#part-4-universal-fault-tolerant-computation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 3 showed that the bivariate-bicycle case study makes memory cheaper and that the bicycle architecture addresses computation at the system level. This part steps back from the BB-specific picture and works through the *universal-gate primitives* that any fault-tolerant computation needs: Clifford operations, a non-Clifford gate, and distillation. The presentation is platform-independent until [Section 4.4](#bb-distillation), which returns to BB codes for one case-study-specific contribution. Memory is only one line of the cost ledger. Computation moves cost into non-Clifford resources, and the real discount shows only once that is summed in. The advantage of the ledger is the whole system, not the single memory line.\n"
msgstr "Ipinakita ng Part 3 na pinapamura ng bivariate-bicycle case study ang memory at na tinutugunan ng bicycle architecture ang computation sa antas ng sistema. Umaatras ang bahaging ito mula sa BB-specific na larawan at tinatalakay ang mga *universal-gate primitive* na kailangan ng anumang fault-tolerant computation: mga Clifford na operasyon, isang non-Clifford gate, at distillation. Platform-independent ang presentasyon hanggang [Section 4.4](#bb-distillation), na bumabalik sa mga BB code para sa isang kontribusyong partikular sa case study. Isa lamang linya ng cost ledger ang memory. Inililipat ng computation ang gastos sa mga non-Clifford na resource, at lumilitaw lamang ang tunay na diskwento kapag naisama ito sa kabuuan. Ang bentahe ng ledger ay ang buong sistema, hindi ang iisang linya ng memory.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The goal here is not to derive magic-state distillation in full, but to understand why protected logical memory is not enough for universal fault-tolerant computation.\n"
msgstr "Ang layunin dito ay hindi ang buong pag-derive ng magic-state distillation, kundi ang maunawaan kung bakit hindi sapat ang protektadong logical memory para sa universal fault-tolerant computation.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "4.1 Clifford structure and the need for non-Clifford resources {#41-clifford-structure-and-the-need-for-non-clifford-resources}"
msgstr "4.1 Clifford structure at ang pangangailangan sa mga non-Clifford na resource {#41-clifford-structure-and-the-need-for-non-clifford-resources}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Before the details, here is the shape of this part in one table:\n"
msgstr "Bago ang mga detalye, narito ang hugis ng bahaging ito sa isang table:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Operation | Why it is easy or hard on a stabilizer code |\n"
"|---|---|\n"
"| Clifford gates ($H$, $S$, CNOT) | Handled relatively naturally, because they preserve Pauli structure |\n"
"| $T$ gate | Provides a non-Clifford resource; implemented here through a *magic state* |\n"
"| Magic-state distillation | Converts many noisy magic states into fewer cleaner ones, at a cost |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Operasyon | Bakit madali o mahirap ito sa isang stabilizer code |\n"
"|---|---|\n"
"| Mga Clifford gate ($H$, $S$, CNOT) | Medyo natural na naha-handle, dahil pinapanatili nila ang Pauli structure |\n"
"| $T$ gate | Nagbibigay ng non-Clifford na resource; ipinapatupad dito sa pamamagitan ng *magic state* |\n"
"| Magic-state distillation | Ginagawang mas kaunti at mas malinis ang maraming maingay na magic state, may kapalit na gastos |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The *Clifford group* $\\mathcal{C}$ is the set of unitaries that map Pauli operators to Pauli operators under conjugation. Standard generators are the Hadamard gate $H$, the phase gate $S = \\mathrm{diag}(1, i)$, and the two-qubit $\\mathrm{CNOT}$. Clifford operations preserve Pauli structure, so encoding, logical Clifford gates, and stabilizer measurement can all be described within this framework. For the surface code, logical Cliffords are implemented by lattice surgery (cutting and joining patches) together with selected *transversal* gates. A transversal gate is applied independently qubit by qubit, with no two qubits of a block interacting, so a single physical fault cannot grow into a high-weight error inside the block. This is what makes such gates automatically fault-tolerant. For BB codes, the bicycle architecture of [Section 3.4](#computation) uses generalized lattice surgery within its logical processing unit, with the same Clifford-friendly structure underneath.\n"
msgstr "Ang *Clifford group* $\\mathcal{C}$ ay ang set ng mga unitary na nagmamapa ng mga Pauli operator sa mga Pauli operator sa ilalim ng conjugation. Ang mga karaniwang generator ay ang Hadamard gate $H$, ang phase gate $S = \\mathrm{diag}(1, i)$, at ang two-qubit na $\\mathrm{CNOT}$. Pinapanatili ng mga Clifford na operasyon ang Pauli structure, kaya ang encoding, mga logical Clifford gate, at stabilizer measurement ay lahat maaaring ilarawan sa loob ng framework na ito. Para sa surface code, ang mga logical Clifford ay ipinapatupad sa pamamagitan ng lattice surgery (pagputol at pagdugtong ng mga patch) kasama ang mga piling *transversal* na gate. Ang transversal na gate ay inilalapat nang hiwalay sa bawat qubit, na walang dalawang qubit ng isang block na nag-iinteract, kaya hindi lalaki ang isang physical fault tungo sa high-weight na error sa loob ng block. Ito ang dahilan kung bakit awtomatikong fault-tolerant ang mga gate na ito. Para sa mga BB code, ginagamit ng bicycle architecture sa [Section 3.4](#computation) ang generalized lattice surgery sa loob ng logical processing unit nito, na may parehong Clifford-friendly na estruktura sa ilalim.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Clifford group alone is not universal for quantum computation. The *Gottesman-Knill theorem* makes the limitation precise. Any quantum circuit using only state preparation in the computational basis, Clifford gates, and computational-basis measurement can be efficiently simulated classically ([Gottesman, 1998](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9807006)). Universal computation therefore requires at least one non-Clifford gate. The standard choice is the $T = \\mathrm{diag}(1, e^{i\\pi/4})$ gate (the $\\pi/8$ gate), which together with the Clifford group generates a dense subgroup of $\\mathrm{SU}(2^n)$. Implementing $T$ fault-tolerantly is the hard step in universal fault-tolerant computation, and the rest of this part walks through how it is done.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"What does &quot;lattice surgery&quot; actually do? (deeper dive)\">\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang Clifford group lamang ay hindi universal para sa quantum computation. Ginagawang tiyak ng *Gottesman-Knill theorem* ang limitasyong ito. Ang anumang quantum circuit na gumagamit lamang ng state preparation sa computational basis, mga Clifford gate, at computational-basis measurement ay maaaring i-simulate nang episyente sa classical na paraan ([Gottesman, 1998](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9807006)). Kaya ang universal computation ay nangangailangan ng kahit isang non-Clifford gate. Ang karaniwang pinipili ay ang $T = \\mathrm{diag}(1, e^{i\\pi/4})$ gate (ang $\\pi/8$ gate), na kasama ng Clifford group ay bumubuo ng dense subgroup ng $\\mathrm{SU}(2^n)$. Ang fault-tolerant na pagpapatupad ng $T$ ang mahirap na hakbang sa universal fault-tolerant computation, at tatalakayin ng natitirang bahagi nito kung paano ito ginagawa.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Ano ba talaga ang ginagawa ng &quot;lattice surgery&quot;? (mas malalim na pagtalakay)\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lattice surgery implements joint logical Pauli measurements by temporarily changing the checks across code patches. For a surface-code logical CNOT with control $C$ and target $T$, one measurement-based description prepares an ancillary logical qubit $A$ in $|+\\rangle_L$, measures $\\bar{Z}_C\\bar{Z}_A$, then $\\bar{X}_A\\bar{X}_T$, and finally $\\bar{Z}_A$, with Pauli-frame updates determined by the three outcomes. A single joint $\\bar{Z}_C\\bar{Z}_T$ measurement only projects onto a parity subspace, and it is not a CNOT. The physical implementation uses repeated syndrome extraction and a layout that maintains error protection throughout the operation. Other logical Clifford operations require related code-deformation or ancillary-state techniques.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapatupad ng lattice surgery ang mga joint logical Pauli measurement sa pamamagitan ng pansamantalang pagbabago ng mga check sa mga code patch. Para sa surface-code logical CNOT na may control $C$ at target $T$, naghahanda ang isang measurement-based na paglalarawan ng ancillary logical qubit $A$ sa $|+\\rangle_L$, sinusukat ang $\\bar{Z}_C\\bar{Z}_A$, pagkatapos ang $\\bar{X}_A\\bar{X}_T$, at panghuli ang $\\bar{Z}_A$, kung saan ang mga Pauli-frame update ay tinutukoy ng tatlong resulta. Ang isang joint $\\bar{Z}_C\\bar{Z}_T$ measurement ay nagppiprojekt lamang sa isang parity subspace, at hindi ito CNOT. Gumagamit ang pisikal na implementasyon ng paulit-ulit na syndrome extraction at isang layout na nagpapanatili ng proteksyon laban sa error sa buong operasyon. Ang ibang logical Clifford na operasyon ay nangangailangan ng kaugnay na code-deformation o ancillary-state na mga teknik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the bicycle architecture, generalized qLDPC surgery and the logical processing unit (LPU) enable selected logical Pauli measurements. Together with the architecture's other instructions, these support logical computation; magic-state injection supplies the non-Clifford resource needed for universality.\n"
msgstr "Sa bicycle architecture, ang generalized qLDPC surgery at ang logical processing unit (LPU) ang nagbibigay-daan sa mga piling logical Pauli measurement. Kasama ng iba pang instruction ng architecture, sinusuportahan ng mga ito ang logical computation; ang magic-state injection ang nagsusuplay ng non-Clifford na resource na kailangan para sa universality.\n"

