# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "4.2 The $T$ gate through magic-state injection {#42-the-t-gate-through-magic-state-injection}"
msgstr "4.2 Ang $T$ gate sa pamamagitan ng magic-state injection {#42-the-t-gate-through-magic-state-injection}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The surface code does not support a transversal logical $T$. The surface-code and BB architectures discussed here implement it through magic-state injection. The approach is to consume a *magic state*, a logical $|A\\rangle = T|+\\rangle = (|0\\rangle + e^{i\\pi/4}|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$ prepared in advance, and perform a Clifford-only circuit that effectively applies $T$ to the data qubit. The circuit is shown below as a static diagram. This lesson treats the conditional Clifford correction that follows the ancilla measurement as a *Clifford-frame update*, not as an active mid-circuit correction.\n"
msgstr "Hindi sinusuportahan ng surface code ang transversal na logical $T$. Ipinapatupad ito ng mga surface-code at BB architecture na tinatalakay dito sa pamamagitan ng magic-state injection. Ang paraan ay ubusin ang isang *magic state*, isang logical $|A\\rangle = T|+\\rangle = (|0\\rangle + e^{i\\pi/4}|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$ na inihanda nang maaga, at magsagawa ng Clifford-only na circuit na epektibong naglalapat ng $T$ sa data qubit. Ipinapakita ang circuit sa ibaba bilang static na diagram. Itinuturing ng lesson na ito ang conditional Clifford correction na sumusunod sa ancilla measurement bilang *Clifford-frame update*, hindi bilang aktibong mid-circuit correction.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/extracted-outputs/cc93812b-0.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/extracted-outputs/cc93812b-0.svg)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The circuit consumes one magic state $|A\\rangle$, applies one $\\mathrm{CNOT}$ between the data and the ancilla, and measures the ancilla in the computational basis, giving a single classical outcome $m \\in \\{0, 1\\}$. That measurement result determines the branch:\n"
msgstr "Gumagamit ang circuit ng isang magic state $|A\\rangle$, naglalapat ng isang $\\mathrm{CNOT}$ sa pagitan ng data at ng ancilla, at sinusukat ang ancilla sa computational basis, na nagbibigay ng iisang classical na resulta $m \\in \\{0, 1\\}$. Ang resulta ng measurement na iyon ang tumutukoy sa branch:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "$m = 0$ branch: the data is in state $T|\\psi\\rangle$ exactly.\n"
msgstr "$m = 0$ na branch: ang data ay nasa state na $T|\\psi\\rangle$ nang eksakto.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- $m = 1$ branch: the data is in state $T^\\dagger|\\psi\\rangle$, up to a global phase. Applying or tracking $S$ gives $ST^\\dagger|\\psi\\rangle = T|\\psi\\rangle$.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Why use T-state injection? (Deeper dive: Eastin-Knill and the Clifford hierarchy)\">\n"
msgstr ""
"- $m = 1$ na branch: ang data ay nasa state na $T^\\dagger|\\psi\\rangle$, hanggang sa global phase. Ang paglalapat o pag-track ng $S$ ay nagbibigay ng $ST^\\dagger|\\psi\\rangle = T|\\psi\\rangle$.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Bakit gagamit ng T-state injection? (Mas malalim na pagtalakay: Eastin-Knill at ang Clifford hierarchy)\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The standard recipe used here (fault-tolerant Clifford gates, with $T$ supplied through injection) is motivated by two structural results.\n"
msgstr "Ang karaniwang recipe na ginagamit dito (mga fault-tolerant na Clifford gate, kung saan ang $T$ ay ibinibigay sa pamamagitan ng injection) ay may motibasyon mula sa dalawang structural na resulta.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**The Clifford hierarchy.** Quantum gates organize into a hierarchy by how they act on Pauli operators under conjugation. Level 1 is the Pauli group. Level 2 is the *Clifford group* $\\mathcal{C}$, which sends Paulis to Paulis. Level 3 contains gates that send Paulis to level-2 elements, and so on.\n"
msgstr "**Ang Clifford hierarchy.** Inoorganisa ang mga quantum gate sa isang hierarchy ayon sa kung paano sila kumikilos sa mga Pauli operator sa ilalim ng conjugation. Ang Level 1 ay ang Pauli group. Ang Level 2 ay ang *Clifford group* $\\mathcal{C}$, na nagpapadala ng mga Pauli sa mga Pauli. Ang Level 3 ay naglalaman ng mga gate na nagpapadala ng mga Pauli sa mga level-2 na elemento, at iba pa.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Level | Examples | Stabilizer code |\n"
"|---|---|---|\n"
"| 1 (Pauli) | $X$, $Y$, $Z$ | Absorbed into the Pauli/Clifford frame |\n"
"| 2 (Clifford) | $H$, $S$, $\\mathrm{CNOT}$ | Transversal or through lattice surgery |\n"
"| 3 | $T$, Toffoli ($\\mathrm{CCX}$) | Often supplied through injection, and transversality depends on the code |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Level | Mga halimbawa | Stabilizer code |\n"
"|---|---|---|\n"
"| 1 (Pauli) | $X$, $Y$, $Z$ | Sinasaklaw ng Pauli/Clifford frame |\n"
"| 2 (Clifford) | $H$, $S$, $\\mathrm{CNOT}$ | Transversal o sa pamamagitan ng lattice surgery |\n"
"| 3 | $T$, Toffoli ($\\mathrm{CCX}$) | Madalas ibinibigay sa pamamagitan ng injection, at nakadepende sa code ang transversality |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "By the Gottesman-Knill theorem, a circuit using only level-2 gates with computational-basis preparation and measurement can be simulated efficiently on a classical computer. Universal quantum computation therefore requires *at least one* non-Clifford resource. The $T$ gate is the canonical choice because it is the simplest such gate and $\\{\\mathcal{C}, T\\}$ is dense in $\\mathrm{SU}(2^n)$, but nothing in the theory forces $T$ over Toffoli or higher-level operations.\n"
msgstr "Ayon sa Gottesman-Knill theorem, ang circuit na gumagamit lamang ng level-2 na mga gate na may computational-basis preparation at measurement ay maaaring i-simulate nang episyente sa classical na computer. Kaya ang universal quantum computation ay nangangailangan ng *kahit isa* na non-Clifford na resource. Ang $T$ gate ang kanonikal na pagpipilian dahil ito ang pinakasimpleng ganoong gate at ang $\\{\\mathcal{C}, T\\}$ ay dense sa $\\mathrm{SU}(2^n)$, pero walang nagtatakda sa teorya na $T$ ang piliin kaysa Toffoli o mas mataas na level na mga operasyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**The Eastin-Knill theorem.** The second result is more constraining. The Eastin-Knill theorem states that a code that detects arbitrary single-qubit errors cannot admit a universal set of *transversal* logical gates ([Eastin and Knill, 2009](https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.4262)). Which gates are transversal depends on the code. The surface-code and BB architectures discussed here use magic-state injection for logical $T$. The transversal set is *not* always the Clifford group. For example, the $[[15,1,3]]$ Reed-Muller code used for distillation in [Section 4.3](#distillation) has a transversal $T$ instead. The point of Eastin-Knill is the restriction on a universal transversal set for a fixed code. An additional fault-tolerant mechanism, such as magic-state injection, is needed for universality.\n"
msgstr "**Ang Eastin-Knill theorem.** Mas mahigpit ang ikalawang resulta. Sinasabi ng Eastin-Knill theorem na ang code na nakaka-detect ng arbitraryong single-qubit na error ay hindi maaaring magkaroon ng universal set ng mga *transversal* na logical gate ([Eastin and Knill, 2009](https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.4262)). Nakadepende sa code kung aling mga gate ang transversal. Gumagamit ang mga surface-code at BB architecture na tinatalakay dito ng magic-state injection para sa logical $T$. Ang transversal set ay *hindi* laging ang Clifford group. Halimbawa, ang $[[15,1,3]]$ Reed-Muller code na ginagamit para sa distillation sa [Section 4.3](#distillation) ay may transversal na $T$ sa halip. Ang punto ng Eastin-Knill ay ang paghihigpit sa universal transversal set para sa isang takdang code. Kailangan ng karagdagang fault-tolerant na mekanismo, tulad ng magic-state injection, para sa universality.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Why injection resolves this.** Injection implements logical $T$ using Clifford operations, measurements, and a supplied logical magic state $|A\\rangle$. Preparing that magic state requires a non-Clifford resource. The state preparation can be done *offline*, decoupled from the moment of consumption. Distillation in [Section 4.3](#distillation) brings that offline preparation up to logical-level fidelity.\n"
msgstr "**Bakit nalulutas ito ng injection.** Ipinapatupad ng injection ang logical $T$ gamit ang mga Clifford na operasyon, mga measurement, at isang ibinigay na logical magic state $|A\\rangle$. Ang paghahanda ng magic state na iyon ay nangangailangan ng non-Clifford na resource. Maaaring gawin ang state preparation *offline*, hiwalay sa sandali ng paggamit. Dinadala ng distillation sa [Section 4.3](#distillation) ang offline na paghahandang iyon sa logical-level na fidelity.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Similar injection methods apply to other non-Clifford gates. Many fault-tolerant resource estimates use *some* magic state, typically $|A\\rangle$ for $T$, $|\\mathrm{CCZ}\\rangle$ for Toffolis, or higher-order states. The choice of $T$ throughout this lesson is the reference convention.\n"
msgstr "Nalalapat ang mga katulad na injection method sa iba pang non-Clifford na gate. Maraming fault-tolerant na resource estimate ang gumagamit ng *ilang* magic state, kadalasan ang $|A\\rangle$ para sa $T$, $|\\mathrm{CCZ}\\rangle$ para sa mga Toffoli, o mas mataas na order na mga state. Ang pagpili ng $T$ sa buong lesson na ito ay ang reference convention.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"The transformation \"perform a logical $T$\" has now become \"supply a high-fidelity logical magic state $|A\\rangle$\", which is a resource-supply problem. The rest of this part addresses the supply.\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Ang pagbabagong \"magsagawa ng logical $T$\" ay naging \"magsuplay ng mataas na fidelity na logical magic state $|A\\rangle$\", na isang problema sa resource supply. Ang natitirang bahagi nito ay tumutugon sa suplay.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"distillation\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "4.3 Magic-state distillation {#43-magic-state-distillation}"
msgstr "4.3 Magic-state distillation {#43-magic-state-distillation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "It is worth pausing on *why* a separate purification step is needed at all. Everything a fault-tolerant computer does cheaply (Clifford gates and stabilizer measurements) is protected directly by the code. The missing ingredient is the non-Clifford $T$ gate. Magic-state injection sidesteps this by consuming a pre-made $|A\\rangle$ state through a Clifford-only circuit, but the difficulty has only moved. The raw injected magic state inherits physical-level error and is far too noisy for a deep logical circuit. A $T$ gate applied through a raw $|A\\rangle$ at fidelity $1 - 10^{-2}$ is useless for a logical circuit aiming at $10^{-12}$ logical error.\n"
msgstr "Sulit na huminto sandali kung *bakit* kailangan pa ng hiwalay na purification step. Lahat ng ginagawa nang mura ng isang fault-tolerant na computer (mga Clifford gate at stabilizer measurement) ay direktang pinoprotektahan ng code. Ang kulang na sangkap ay ang non-Clifford na $T$ gate. Iniiwasan ito ng magic-state injection sa pamamagitan ng paggamit ng handa nang $|A\\rangle$ state sa pamamagitan ng Clifford-only na circuit, pero lumipat lang ang kahirapan. Namamana ng hilaw na na-inject na magic state ang physical-level na error at napakaingay nito para sa malalim na logical circuit. Ang $T$ gate na inilapat sa pamamagitan ng hilaw na $|A\\rangle$ na may fidelity na $1 - 10^{-2}$ ay walang silbi para sa logical circuit na may target na $10^{-12}$ na logical error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Distillation* is the standard way out. It takes many noisy magic states as input and produces fewer high-fidelity ones as output, using only Clifford operations and measurement, which the underlying code natively supports. The most studied protocol is *15-to-1 distillation* ([Bravyi and Kitaev, 2005](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0403025)). Fifteen noisy magic states at error probability $p$ yield one output at $\\sim 35\\,p^3$, using the $[[15,1,3]]$ Reed-Muller code. (The $35$ counts the leading undetected weight-3 error patterns.)\n"
"The cubic suppression $p \\to 35\\,p^3$ is iterated over rounds. Starting from $p = 10^{-2}$ raw error, one round gives $\\sim 3.5 \\times 10^{-5}$, and two rounds give $\\sim 1.5 \\times 10^{-12}$. Each round multiplies the raw-state cost by 15, so reaching deep error targets is expensive in the number of input states required.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang *Distillation* ang karaniwang solusyon. Kumukuha ito ng maraming maingay na magic state bilang input at gumagawa ng mas kaunting mataas na fidelity bilang output, gamit lamang ang mga Clifford na operasyon at measurement, na likas na sinusuportahan ng pinagbabatayang code. Ang pinakamaraming pinag-aralang protocol ay ang *15-to-1 distillation* ([Bravyi and Kitaev, 2005](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0403025)). Labinlimang maingay na magic state na may error probability na $p$ ang nagbubunga ng isang output sa $\\sim 35\\,p^3$, gamit ang $[[15,1,3]]$ Reed-Muller code. (Binibilang ng $35$ ang mga nangungunang undetected na weight-3 error pattern.)\n"
"Ang cubic suppression na $p \\to 35\\,p^3$ ay inuulit sa mga round. Simula sa $p = 10^{-2}$ na raw error, ang isang round ay nagbibigay ng $\\sim 3.5 \\times 10^{-5}$, at ang dalawang round ay nagbibigay ng $\\sim 1.5 \\times 10^{-12}$. Pinaparami ng bawat round ang gastos ng raw state ng 15, kaya magastos sa dami ng kinakailangang input state ang pag-abot sa malalalim na error target.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For a surface-code-based fault-tolerant computer, *magic-state factories* (separate code patches running distillation rounds in parallel with the main computation) typically take a substantial fraction of the total physical-qubit budget at the logical error targets of interest. Optimized layouts can trim this considerably ([Litinski, 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.06903)). Higher-yield protocols such as triorthogonal codes ([Bravyi and Haah, 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.2426)) reduce the cost ratio but do not change the overall structure. Distillation is a resource process that runs alongside the main computation and pays for its own qubits.\n"
msgstr "Para sa fault-tolerant na computer na nakabatay sa surface code, ang *magic-state factories* (hiwalay na mga code patch na nagpapatakbo ng mga distillation round nang kasabay ng pangunahing computation) ay karaniwang kumukuha ng malaking bahagi ng kabuuang physical-qubit budget sa mga logical error target na mahalaga. Maaaring bawasan nang malaki ng mga na-optimize na layout ito ([Litinski, 2019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.06903)). Ang mga protocol na mas mataas ang yield tulad ng triorthogonal code ([Bravyi and Haah, 2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.2426)) ay nagpapababa ng cost ratio pero hindi nagbabago sa pangkalahatang estruktura. Ang distillation ay isang resource process na tumatakbo kasabay ng pangunahing computation at nagbabayad para sa sarili nitong mga qubit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"bb-distillation\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "4.4 Distillation on BB codes {#44-distillation-on-bb-codes}"
msgstr "4.4 Distillation sa mga BB code {#44-distillation-on-bb-codes}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The bivariate-bicycle study adds a twist. Because the gross code packs 12 logical qubits into 288 physical qubits, the block's own high-rate logical capacity can be turned on distillation. [Xu et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20546) build magic-state factories that run *inside a single BB code block* through qubit recycling, while the bicycle architecture of [Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094) also supplies non-Clifford resources through dedicated factory modules using surface-code protocols for *cultivation* ([Gidney et al., 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.17595)) or distillation, depending on the physical-error regime. Either way, magic-state preparation is *not* free. In-block or in a factory, it still costs logical capacity, time, routing, measurement, and decoding. The rate advantage moves and shrinks that cost rather than removing it.\n"
"| Scheme | Logical-qubit use | Footprint | Note |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| Dedicated surface-code factory | Separate factory patches | Large additional region | Factory is spatially separated |\n"
"| Single-block factory on gross $[[144,12,12]]$ | Reuses some of the 12 logical qubits over time | Reduced additional footprint | *Not free*, since it pays in scheduling, time, routing, and control |\n"
msgstr ""
"Nagdaragdag ng twist ang bivariate-bicycle study. Dahil isinisiksik ng gross code ang 12 logical qubit sa 288 physical qubit, ang sariling high-rate na logical capacity ng block ay maaaring gamitin sa distillation. Bumubuo ang [Xu et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20546) ng mga magic-state factory na tumatakbo *sa loob ng isang BB code block* sa pamamagitan ng qubit recycling, habang ang bicycle architecture ng [Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094) ay nagsusuplay rin ng mga non-Clifford na resource sa pamamagitan ng nakalaang factory module na gumagamit ng mga surface-code protocol para sa *cultivation* ([Gidney et al., 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.17595)) o distillation, depende sa physical-error regime. Sa alinmang paraan, *hindi* libre ang magic-state preparation. Sa loob ng block man o sa factory, may gastos pa rin ito sa logical capacity, oras, routing, measurement, at decoding. Inililipat at pinaliliit ng rate advantage ang gastos na iyon sa halip na alisin ito.\n"
"| Scheme | Paggamit ng logical qubit | Footprint | Tala |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| Nakalaang surface-code factory | Hiwalay na mga factory patch | Malaking karagdagang rehiyon | Hiwalay sa espasyo ang factory |\n"
"| Single-block factory sa gross $[[144,12,12]]$ | Muling ginagamit ang ilan sa 12 logical qubit sa paglipas ng panahon | Mas maliit na karagdagang footprint | *Hindi libre*, dahil nagbabayad ito sa scheduling, oras, routing, at control |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Qualitative comparison; see [Xu et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20546) and [Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094) for specifics.*\n"
msgstr "*Kwalitatibong paghahambing; tingnan ang [Xu et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20546) at [Yoder et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03094) para sa mga detalye.*\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The $15^r$ growth in input states is one part of the cost. Each output magic state at a deep error target consumes hundreds to thousands of raw ones, and those raw states must themselves be produced, stored, and processed somewhere on the chip.\n"
msgstr "Ang $15^r$ na paglaki ng mga input state ay isang bahagi ng gastos. Ang bawat output na magic state sa malalim na error target ay kumokonsumo ng daan-daan hanggang libu-libong raw na state, at ang mga raw na state na iyon ay kailangan ding gawin, itago, at iproseso sa kung saan sa chip.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The single-block factory result is specific to the BB/gross-code construction, not a general property of all qLDPC codes. For codes with lower rate, dedicated factories remain natural. The broader point is that the *rate advantage* of high-rate qLDPC codes compounds. The same advantage that reduces memory overhead in [Section 3.2](#gross-memory) can also lower distillation overhead, since high-rate logical capacity can be brought to bear on it, whether through the single-block factories of Xu et al. 2026 (above) or the dedicated factory modules that Yoder et al. 2025 develop.\n"
msgstr "Ang resulta ng single-block factory ay partikular sa BB/gross-code construction, hindi pangkalahatang katangian ng lahat ng qLDPC code. Para sa mga code na mas mababa ang rate, natural pa ring gamitin ang mga nakalaang factory. Ang mas malawak na punto ay ang *rate advantage* ng mga high-rate na qLDPC code ay nagsasama-sama. Ang parehong bentahe na nagpapababa ng memory overhead sa [Section 3.2](#gross-memory) ay maaari ring magpababa ng distillation overhead, dahil maaaring gamitin dito ang high-rate na logical capacity, sa pamamagitan man ng mga single-block factory ng Xu et al. 2026 (sa itaas) o ng mga nakalaang factory module na binuo ng Yoder et al. 2025.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "4.5 What this part establishes and what it does not prove {#45-what-this-part-establishes-and-what-it-does-not-prove}"
msgstr "4.5 Ang napapatunayan at hindi napapatunayan ng bahaging ito {#45-what-this-part-establishes-and-what-it-does-not-prove}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The architectures discussed here combine the same building blocks: fault-tolerant Clifford operations, a non-Clifford resource (the $T$ gate, through magic states), and a distillation protocol to bring magic-state fidelity to the level the logical computation requires. Part 4 walked through these primitives in their general form, with one specialization to the BB case study (distillation on high-rate BB codes).\n"
msgstr "Pinagsasama ng mga architecture na tinatalakay dito ang parehong building block: mga fault-tolerant na Clifford na operasyon, isang non-Clifford na resource (ang $T$ gate, sa pamamagitan ng mga magic state), at isang distillation protocol para dalhin ang magic-state fidelity sa antas na kailangan ng logical computation. Tinalakay ng Part 4 ang mga primitive na ito sa pangkalahatang anyo, na may isang espesyalisasyon para sa BB case study (distillation sa mga high-rate na BB code).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "What this part does not prove is that the canonical choices (15-to-1 distillation, $T$-state injection, and lattice surgery for Clifford gates) are optimal for any specific algorithm. Alternative constructions exist. Triorthogonal codes for higher-yield distillation, surgery generalizations, and other non-Clifford resource constructions are active research areas. This lesson treats the canonical pipeline as a *reference framework* for discussing BB-code resource estimates, and quantifying alternatives is its own line of work.\n"
msgstr "Ang hindi napapatunayan ng bahaging ito ay ang mga kanonikal na pagpipilian (15-to-1 distillation, $T$-state injection, at lattice surgery para sa mga Clifford gate) ay pinakamainam para sa anumang partikular na algorithm. May mga alternatibong konstruksyon. Ang mga triorthogonal code para sa mas mataas na yield na distillation, mga generalization ng surgery, at iba pang non-Clifford na resource construction ay aktibong larangan ng pananaliksik. Itinuturing ng lesson na ito ang kanonikal na pipeline bilang *reference framework* sa pagtalakay ng mga BB-code resource estimate, at ang pagsukat ng mga alternatibo ay sarili nitong linya ng gawain.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-3}"
msgstr "Suriin ang iyong pag-unawa {#check-your-understanding-3}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Does magic-state distillation increase or decrease the number of magic states?\n"
msgstr "Pinapataas ba o pinababa ng magic-state distillation ang bilang ng mga magic state?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Distillation decreases it. Many noisy states go in, and fewer clean ones come out (for 15-to-1, fifteen in and one out per round). It buys *fidelity* with *quantity*.\n"
msgstr "Pinababa nito. Maraming maingay na state ang pumapasok, at mas kaunting malilinis ang lumalabas (para sa 15-to-1, labinlima ang pumapasok at isa ang lumalabas bawat round). Binibili nito ang *fidelity* gamit ang *dami*.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Injection already lets you apply a $T$ gate by consuming an $|A\\rangle$ state plus a Clifford circuit. So why is distillation still needed?\n"
msgstr "Pinapayagan ka na ng injection na maglapat ng $T$ gate sa pamamagitan ng pagkonsumo ng $|A\\rangle$ state at Clifford circuit. Kaya bakit kailangan pa rin ang distillation?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A *raw* $|A\\rangle$ prepared from physical qubits is far too noisy. A fidelity of $\\sim 1-10^{-2}$ is useless for a circuit targeting $10^{-12}$ logical error. Distillation spends many noisy $|A\\rangle$ plus Clifford-only operations to produce fewer, much cleaner ones ($p \\to 35p^3$ per 15-to-1 round). It does not replace the non-Clifford resource. It *supplies* that resource at usable fidelity. (It consumes Cliffords rather than returning to them, it acts on the magic states rather than on data-qubit errors, and it makes preparation longer, not shorter.)\n"
msgstr "Ang *raw* na $|A\\rangle$ na inihanda mula sa mga physical qubit ay masyadong maingay. Ang fidelity na $\\sim 1-10^{-2}$ ay walang silbi para sa circuit na may target na $10^{-12}$ na logical error. Gumagastos ang distillation ng maraming maingay na $|A\\rangle$ at Clifford-only na mga operasyon para makagawa ng mas kaunti at mas malilinis na state ($p \\to 35p^3$ bawat 15-to-1 round). Hindi nito pinapalitan ang non-Clifford na resource. *Isinusuplay* nito ang resource na iyon sa magagamit na fidelity. (Kumokonsumo ito ng mga Clifford sa halip na bumalik sa mga ito, kumikilos ito sa mga magic state sa halip na sa mga error ng data-qubit, at pinahahaba nito ang preparation, hindi pinaiikli.)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 5: Quantum error correction as a quantum-classical system {#part-5-quantum-error-correction-as-a-quantum-classical-system}"
msgstr "Part 5: Quantum error correction bilang quantum-classical na sistema {#part-5-quantum-error-correction-as-a-quantum-classical-system}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Parts 3 and 4 treated QEC primarily at the code level, covering what stabilizer structure a code has, what gates the code supports, and what overheads its operations require. A real fault-tolerant computer has to do more than satisfy these code-level requirements. It has to read out syndromes in real time, decode them on classical hardware fast enough to keep up with the QEC cycle, route logical operations between code blocks, and integrate everything into modular hardware that can be fabricated. This part walks through the system-level pieces, with the IBM superconducting case study as the running example. The qubit savings of Part 3 reappear here as classical-compute (decoding) and hardware (connectivity, architecture) cost. The ledger includes the decoder and the wiring, not just the code.\n"
"<span id=\"decoder\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Tinrato ng Parts 3 at 4 ang QEC pangunahin sa antas ng code, kung anong stabilizer structure ang mayroon ang isang code, anong mga gate ang sinusuportahan nito, at anong mga overhead ang kailangan ng mga operasyon nito. Ang totoong fault-tolerant na computer ay kailangang gumawa ng higit pa sa pagtugon sa mga code-level na requirement na ito. Kailangan nitong basahin ang mga syndrome sa real time, i-decode ang mga ito sa classical na hardware nang sapat na bilis para makasabay sa QEC cycle, i-route ang mga logical na operasyon sa pagitan ng mga code block, at isama ang lahat sa modular na hardware na maaaring gawin. Tinatalakay ng bahaging ito ang mga system-level na piraso, gamit ang IBM superconducting case study bilang tuloy-tuloy na halimbawa. Lumilitaw muli rito ang pagtitipid sa qubit ng Part 3 bilang gastos sa classical-compute (decoding) at hardware (connectivity, architecture). Kasama sa ledger ang decoder at ang wiring, hindi lang ang code.\n"
"<span id=\"decoder\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "5.1 The decoder as the classical half of QEC {#51-the-decoder-as-the-classical-half-of-qec}"
msgstr "5.1 Ang decoder bilang classical na kalahati ng QEC {#51-the-decoder-as-the-classical-half-of-qec}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A decoder solves a classical inference problem. Given a noisy syndrome history, it infers a likely recovery class without learning the protected logical state.\n"
msgstr "Nilulutas ng decoder ang isang classical inference problem. Mula sa isang maingay na syndrome history, hinuhulaan nito ang malamang na recovery class nang hindi natututunan ang protektadong logical state.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When a stabilizer code's checks are measured, the result is a classical bitstring called the *syndrome*, indicating which checks fire. The job of the *decoder* is to take this syndrome and infer which error most likely occurred, so that the inferred error can be accounted for in the logical interpretation, usually by updating a classical Pauli/Clifford frame rather than by physically undoing the error in the notebook. The decoder is therefore *classical*. It runs on conventional hardware, such as field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs), application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs), GPUs, or CPUs, and is part of the quantum-classical loop without which a stabilizer code does not protect any information.\n"
msgstr "Kapag sinusukat ang mga check ng isang stabilizer code, ang resulta ay isang classical na bitstring na tinatawag na *syndrome*, na nagpapakita kung aling mga check ang nag-fire. Ang trabaho ng *decoder* ay kunin ang syndrome na ito at hulaan kung aling error ang pinakamalamang na nangyari, para maisaalang-alang ang hinulaang error sa logical interpretation, kadalasan sa pamamagitan ng pag-update ng classical na Pauli/Clifford frame sa halip na pisikal na bawiin ang error sa notebook. Kaya *classical* ang decoder. Tumatakbo ito sa karaniwang hardware, tulad ng field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs), application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs), GPUs, o CPUs, at bahagi ito ng quantum-classical loop na kung wala ay hindi nagpoprotekta ng anumang impormasyon ang stabilizer code.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The simplest possible decoder is a *lookup table*, a function from syndromes (bitstrings) to most-likely errors (Pauli strings). For codes with very few checks this is trivial, but the lookup-table approach does not scale. The number of entries grows as $2^{m}$ in the number of checks $m$, as the next cell demonstrates.\n"
msgstr "Ang pinakasimpleng posibleng decoder ay isang *lookup table*, isang function mula sa mga syndrome (bitstring) patungo sa mga pinakamalamang na error (Pauli string). Para sa mga code na kaunti lang ang check, simple lang ito, pero hindi nag-i-scale ang lookup-table na approach. Ang bilang ng mga entry ay lumalaki bilang $2^{m}$ sa bilang ng mga check na $m$, gaya ng ipapakita ng susunod na cell.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The table above makes the scaling concrete. The three-qubit repetition code has two checks, so its table has four entries. Toy surface patches have around 50 checks, requiring $2^{50} \\approx 10^{15}$ entries, and gross-code-scale qLDPC blocks have hundreds of checks, requiring tables far larger than any feasible classical memory. Practical decoders therefore *infer* rather than enumerate. They parameterize the most-likely-error problem and solve it algorithmically.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapakita ng talahanayan sa itaas kung gaano kabilis lumaki ang scaling. Ang three-qubit repetition code ay may dalawang check, kaya apat ang entry ng talahanayan nito. Ang mga toy surface patch ay may humigit-kumulang 50 check, kaya kailangan nila ng $2^{50} \\approx 10^{15}$ na entry, at ang mga qLDPC block na kasinlaki ng gross code ay may daan-daang check, kaya kailangan nila ng mga talahanayang mas malaki kaysa sa anumang magagawang classical memory. Kaya ang mga praktikal na decoder ay *nag-i-infer* sa halip na magbilang ng lahat. Ginagawa nilang parameter ang problema ng most-likely-error at nilulutas ito gamit ang algorithm.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"decoder-families\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "5.2 Decoder families across code families {#52-decoder-families-across-code-families}"
msgstr "5.2 Mga pamilya ng decoder sa iba't ibang pamilya ng code {#52-decoder-families-across-code-families}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Surface codes are decoded primarily by *minimum-weight perfect matching* (MWPM). Syndrome defects come in pairs at the endpoints of error strings, and the minimum-weight pairing yields the most-likely-error inference. MWPM runs in polynomial time ([blossom algorithm for minimum-weight matching](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blossom_algorithm)), has been engineered into FPGA implementations achieving real-time latency, and is the mature reference baseline for fault-tolerant prototypes.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga surface code ay pangunahing dinedecode gamit ang *minimum-weight perfect matching* (MWPM). Ang mga syndrome defect ay nagkakapares sa mga dulo ng error string, at ang minimum-weight na pagpapares ang nagbibigay ng most-likely-error inference. Tumatakbo ang MWPM sa polynomial time ([blossom algorithm para sa minimum-weight matching](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blossom_algorithm)), nagawa na itong FPGA implementation na umaabot sa real-time latency, at ito ang mature na reference baseline para sa mga fault-tolerant prototype.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Standard minimum-weight matching does not apply directly to bivariate-bicycle codes. A single Pauli error can trigger more than two syndrome bits, so the most-likely-error problem is no longer plain matching. The standard qLDPC decoder is *belief propagation* with *ordered-statistics decoding* (BP + OSD). BP infers the likely error by passing messages on the code's *Tanner graph* (the bipartite graph with a node for every qubit and every check, and an edge wherever a check touches a qubit), and OSD cleans up the cases where BP fails to converge. BP + OSD is accurate but can be too slow for a real-time loop.\n"
msgstr "Hindi direktang magagamit ang karaniwang minimum-weight matching sa bivariate-bicycle code. Ang isang Pauli error ay maaaring mag-trigger ng higit sa dalawang syndrome bit, kaya hindi na simpleng matching ang problema ng most-likely-error. Ang karaniwang qLDPC decoder ay ang *belief propagation* kasama ang *ordered-statistics decoding* (BP + OSD). Ini-infer ng BP ang malamang na error sa pamamagitan ng pagpapasa ng mga mensahe sa *Tanner graph* ng code (ang bipartite graph na may node para sa bawat qubit at bawat check, at isang edge saanman humahawak ang isang check sa isang qubit), at inaayos ng OSD ang mga kasong hindi nagko-converge ang BP. Tumpak ang BP + OSD pero maaaring masyadong mabagal para sa real-time na loop.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Relay-BP* ([Müller et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01779)) is a recent IBM proposal that speeds up BP's convergence to address this latency. Speed matters because a superconducting QEC cycle produces a new syndrome roughly every 1 µs. If the decoder cannot keep pace *on average*, unprocessed syndromes pile up (the *backlog problem*), and the logical error climbs. A better code alone cannot rescue a decoder that falls behind. The binding requirement is therefore not just accuracy but keeping the average decode time within the per-cycle budget, so decoder latency is a *systems requirement*. An FPGA prototype later brought the per-cycle decode latency under this budget ([Maurer et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21600)).\n"
"<span id=\"modular-architecture\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang *Relay-BP* ([Müller et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01779)) ay isang bagong panukala ng IBM na nagpapabilis ng convergence ng BP para matugunan ang latency na ito. Mahalaga ang bilis dahil ang isang superconducting QEC cycle ay gumagawa ng bagong syndrome humigit-kumulang bawat 1 µs. Kung hindi makasabay ang decoder *sa karaniwan*, mag-iipon ang mga hindi pa napoprosesong syndrome (ang *backlog problem*), at tataas ang logical error. Hindi maililigtas ng mas magandang code lang ang decoder na naiiwan. Kaya ang pangunahing requirement ay hindi lang katumpakan kundi ang pagpapanatili ng karaniwang oras ng decode sa loob ng budget kada cycle, kaya ang decoder latency ay isang *systems requirement*. Kalaunan, napababa ng isang FPGA prototype ang decode latency kada cycle sa ilalim ng budget na ito ([Maurer et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21600)).\n"
"<span id=\"modular-architecture\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "5.3 Modular architecture: connectivity, couplers, and chip lineage {#53-modular-architecture-connectivity-couplers-and-chip-lineage}"
msgstr "5.3 Modular na arkitektura: connectivity, mga coupler, at lineage ng chip {#53-modular-architecture-connectivity-couplers-and-chip-lineage}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The decoder is the classical half of the system, and the *connectivity graph* of the physical qubits is the quantum half. The cost moved out of physical-qubit count in [Section 3.2](#gross-memory) lands here. The weight-6 checks of BB codes include long-range edges that ordinary 2D nearest-neighbor hardware cannot provide. The chip-level answer is a coupler that reaches across the chip.\n"
"![C-couplers enable long-range connections between distant qubits within a quantum chip](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/c-couplers.avif)\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang decoder ang classical na kalahati ng sistema, at ang *connectivity graph* ng mga pisikal na qubit ang quantum na kalahati. Dito napupunta ang gastos na inilipat palabas ng bilang ng pisikal na qubit sa [Section 3.2](#gross-memory). Kasama sa mga weight-6 check ng BB code ang mga long-range na edge na hindi kayang ibigay ng karaniwang 2D nearest-neighbor na hardware. Ang sagot sa antas ng chip ay isang coupler na umaabot sa kabilang bahagi ng chip.\n"
"![Ang mga C-coupler ay nagbibigay-daan sa mga long-range na koneksyon sa pagitan ng magkakalayong qubit sa loob ng isang quantum chip](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-ii/c-couplers.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*C-couplers, which couple qubits more distant than their nearest neighbors, enable long-range connections within a single quantum chip. This is the hardware-level answer to the \"connectivity\" cost flagged in the paragraph above. Where the surface code needs only nearest-neighbor couplers, gross-code-style codes require a few longer-range connections. The check graph is a degree-6 layout of two edge-disjoint planar subgraphs, mostly local with some long-range edges. The IBM Quantum Loon processor is an experimental chip incorporating c-couplers, built specifically for proof-of-concept experiments with high-rate qLDPC codes. Source: [IBM fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc).*\n"
"The c-coupler figure above shows one such hardware response. Other platforms address the same connectivity challenge differently, as [Section 5.4](#other-platforms) sketches. In the IBM modular architecture, inter-module l-couplers connect separately fabricated chips and support universal adapters between modules. This provides a route to scaling memory and processing without requiring all components on one chip. The fault-tolerance milestones in the IBM roadmap are summarized below.\n"
"| Roadmap year | Chip or system | Capability or milestone | Why it matters for BB |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| 2025 | Loon | Long-range c-coupler demonstrator | Tests connectivity for non-local checks |\n"
"| 2026 | Kookaburra | qLDPC memory with an attached LPU (target) | Combines storage and logical processing |\n"
"| 2027 | Cockatoo | Inter-module entanglement (target) | Connects modules through universal adapters |\n"
"| 2028 | Starling proof-of-concept | Magic-state injection with multiple modules (target) | Integrates components for universal computation |\n"
"| 2029 | Starling | Large-scale fault tolerance (target) | Scales to 200 logical qubits and 100 million gates |\n"
msgstr ""
"*Ang mga C-coupler, na nagkokonekta ng mga qubit na mas malayo kaysa sa pinakamalapit na kapitbahay, ay nagbibigay-daan sa mga long-range na koneksyon sa loob ng iisang quantum chip. Ito ang sagot sa antas ng hardware sa gastos sa \"connectivity\" na binanggit sa talata sa itaas. Kung ang surface code ay nangangailangan lang ng nearest-neighbor na mga coupler, ang mga code na kagaya ng gross code ay nangangailangan ng ilang mas long-range na koneksyon. Ang check graph ay isang degree-6 na layout ng dalawang edge-disjoint na planar subgraph, na karamihan ay lokal at may ilang long-range na edge. Ang IBM Quantum Loon processor ay isang eksperimental na chip na may mga c-coupler, na ginawa partikular para sa mga proof-of-concept na eksperimento gamit ang high-rate na qLDPC code. Pinagmulan: [IBM fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc).*\n"
"Ipinapakita ng c-coupler na figure sa itaas ang isang ganitong tugon sa hardware. Iba ang paraan ng ibang platform sa paglutas ng parehong hamon sa connectivity, ayon sa ibinabalangkas ng [Section 5.4](#other-platforms). Sa modular na arkitektura ng IBM, ang mga inter-module na l-coupler ay nagkokonekta ng mga hiwa-hiwalay na ginawang chip at sumusuporta sa mga universal adapter sa pagitan ng mga module. Nagbibigay ito ng daan para i-scale ang memory at processing nang hindi kailangang ilagay ang lahat ng component sa iisang chip. Ang mga fault-tolerance milestone sa IBM roadmap ay nakabuod sa ibaba.\n"
"| Taon sa roadmap | Chip o sistema | Kakayahan o milestone | Bakit mahalaga para sa BB |\n"
"|---|---|---|---|\n"
"| 2025 | Loon | Long-range c-coupler demonstrator | Sinusubok ang connectivity para sa mga non-local na check |\n"
"| 2026 | Kookaburra | qLDPC memory na may kasamang LPU (target) | Pinagsasama ang storage at logical processing |\n"
"| 2027 | Cockatoo | Inter-module entanglement (target) | Ikinokonekta ang mga module gamit ang mga universal adapter |\n"
"| 2028 | Starling proof-of-concept | Magic-state injection gamit ang maraming module (target) | Pinagsasama ang mga component para sa universal computation |\n"
"| 2029 | Starling | Large-scale fault tolerance (target) | Nag-i-scale hanggang 200 logical qubit at 100 milyong gate |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Years and milestones follow the IBM [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc). Target entries describe planned capabilities.*\n"
"<span id=\"other-platforms\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"*Ang mga taon at milestone ay batay sa IBM [fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc). Ang mga target na entry ay naglalarawan ng mga planadong kakayahan.*\n"
"<span id=\"other-platforms\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "5.4 Other platforms {#54-other-platforms}"
msgstr "5.4 Iba pang platform {#54-other-platforms}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The systems perspective also clarifies why different hardware platforms naturally lean toward different code families.\n"
"| Platform | Strength | Example QEC approaches |\n"
"|---|---|---|\n"
"| Superconducting | Fast gates, mature control | Surface baseline; qLDPC through long-range c-couplers |\n"
"| Neutral atoms | Large arrays, reconfigurable geometry | Natural for sparse/non-local (hypergraph-product, HGP-style) layouts |\n"
"| Trapped ions | High fidelity, flexible connectivity | Small-code demonstrations, modular paths |\n"
"| Photonics | Networking, measurement-based | Fusion-based fault-tolerant architectures |\n"
"The matching is not a ranking. Each platform has its own connectivity affordances and dominant error mechanisms, and the code family that fits best there reflects those affordances. Trapped-ion systems can support high-fidelity all-to-all gates within an ion trap, which can facilitate non-local checks. Scaling across traps adds the cost of ion transport or inter-module links. Neutral atoms support reconfigurable geometries (atoms can be moved between operations), which suits sparse non-local codes, including hypergraph-product and BB-style constructions. Photonic approaches include fusion-based and measurement-based protocols, in which error syndromes are reconstructed from combinations of measurement outcomes, including fusion outcomes ([Bartolucci et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.09310)).\n"
msgstr ""
"Nililinaw din ng systems perspective kung bakit natural na umaangkop ang iba't ibang hardware platform sa iba't ibang pamilya ng code.\n"
"| Platform | Lakas | Halimbawang QEC approach |\n"
"|---|---|---|\n"
"| Superconducting | Mabibilis na gate, mature na control | Surface baseline; qLDPC sa pamamagitan ng long-range na c-coupler |\n"
"| Neutral atoms | Malalaking array, reconfigurable na geometry | Natural para sa sparse/non-local (hypergraph-product, HGP-style) na layout |\n"
"| Trapped ions | Mataas na fidelity, flexible na connectivity | Maliliit na demonstrasyon ng code, modular na landas |\n"
"| Photonics | Networking, measurement-based | Fusion-based na fault-tolerant na arkitektura |\n"
"Hindi ranking ang pagtutugma. May kanya-kanyang connectivity affordance at nangingibabaw na mekanismo ng error ang bawat platform, at ang pamilya ng code na pinakaangkop doon ay sumasalamin sa mga affordance na iyon. Kayang suportahan ng mga trapped-ion system ang high-fidelity na all-to-all na gate sa loob ng isang ion trap, na makakatulong sa mga non-local na check. Ang pag-scale sa maraming trap ay nagdaragdag ng gastos ng ion transport o mga inter-module na link. Sinusuportahan ng mga neutral atom ang mga reconfigurable na geometry (maaaring ilipat ang mga atom sa pagitan ng mga operasyon), na bagay sa mga sparse na non-local na code, kasama ang hypergraph-product at BB-style na konstruksyon. Kabilang sa mga photonic approach ang mga fusion-based at measurement-based na protocol, kung saan ang mga error syndrome ay binubuo mula sa kombinasyon ng mga resulta ng measurement, kasama ang mga resulta ng fusion ([Bartolucci et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.09310)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The point is not that one of these is the \"right\" platform; rather, it is that fault-tolerance designs optimize the *code-decoder-hardware triple*, and different points in that space lead to different sensible code choices. The fault-tolerance bottleneck moves around, but the systems-level structure is platform-independent.\n"
msgstr "Hindi ang punto na may isa sa mga ito na ang \"tamang\" platform; sa halip, ino-optimize ng mga disenyo ng fault-tolerance ang *code-decoder-hardware triple*, at ang iba't ibang punto sa espasyong iyon ay humahantong sa iba't ibang makatwirang pagpili ng code. Gumagalaw ang bottleneck ng fault-tolerance, pero ang istruktura sa antas ng sistema ay hindi nakadepende sa platform.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "5.5 Summary {#55-summary}"
msgstr "5.5 Buod {#55-summary}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "QEC requires a classical decoder, a connectivity graph that the code's checks can be implemented on, and a system architecture that integrates both with the quantum hardware. The IBM superconducting case study (the chip lineage tabulated in [Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) gives a concrete sequence of milestones for that integration on one platform.\n"
msgstr "Kailangan ng QEC ng classical decoder, ng connectivity graph kung saan maipapatupad ang mga check ng code, at ng arkitektura ng sistema na nagsasama sa dalawa sa quantum hardware. Ang case study ng IBM superconducting (ang chip lineage na nakatala sa [Section 5.3](#modular-architecture)) ay nagbibigay ng konkretong sunud-sunod na milestone para sa integrasyong iyon sa isang platform.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This does not mean we have achieved fault-tolerant quantum computing at scale. There are still hardware innovations required, as outlined on the IBM Quantum Roadmap. Also, questions related to decoder complexity and speed are the subject of current research. Fortunately, we do not need to wait; we can leverage quantum error suppression, mitigation, and basic correction today, while working toward large-scale fault-tolerant quantum computing.\n"
msgstr "Hindi ibig sabihin nito na naabot na natin ang fault-tolerant quantum computing sa malakihang antas. Kailangan pa rin ng mga inobasyon sa hardware, gaya ng nakasaad sa IBM Quantum Roadmap. Gayundin, ang mga tanong tungkol sa pagiging kumplikado at bilis ng decoder ay paksa pa ng kasalukuyang pananaliksik. Mabuti na lang, hindi natin kailangang maghintay; magagamit na natin ngayon ang quantum error suppression, mitigation, at basic correction, habang pinagsisikapan ang malakihang fault-tolerant quantum computing.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-4}"
msgstr "Suriin ang iyong pag-unawa {#check-your-understanding-4}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lookup-table decoding scales as $2^{(\\text{number of checks})}$. It is fine for the three-qubit repetition code (two checks). Why does it fail for the gross code ($\\sim 144$ checks), and what replaces it?\n"
msgstr "Ang lookup-table decoding ay nag-i-scale bilang $2^{(\\text{number of checks})}$. Okay lang ito para sa three-qubit repetition code (dalawang check). Bakit ito pumapalya para sa gross code ($\\sim 144$ check), at ano ang pumapalit dito?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The gross code would need $2^{144} \\approx 10^{43}$ table entries, far beyond any table one could feasibly store. So practical decoders *infer* the most likely error algorithmically instead of enumerating syndromes, using MWPM for the surface code, BP+OSD for BB codes, and Relay-BP for real-time qLDPC decoding. (The syndrome is still a classical bitstring. The obstacle is the exponential *size* of the syndrome space, not its type.)\n"
msgstr "Ang gross code ay mangangailangan ng $2^{144} \\approx 10^{43}$ na entry sa talahanayan, na higit pa sa anumang talahanayang maiimbak. Kaya ang mga praktikal na decoder ay *nag-i-infer* ng pinakamalamang na error gamit ang algorithm sa halip na magbilang ng mga syndrome, gamit ang MWPM para sa surface code, BP+OSD para sa mga BB code, at Relay-BP para sa real-time na qLDPC decoding. (Classical bitstring pa rin ang syndrome. Ang hadlang ay ang exponential na *laki* ng syndrome space, hindi ang uri nito.)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Consider a superconducting QEC cycle that yields a new syndrome every 1 µs. Why must the decoder keep pace *on average*, and what breaks if it cannot?\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Isipin ang isang superconducting QEC cycle na nagbibigay ng bagong syndrome bawat 1 µs. Bakit kailangang makasabay ang decoder *sa karaniwan*, at ano ang masisira kung hindi?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The decoder must process syndrome data at least as fast as it arrives. Otherwise, a backlog grows and operations that depend on decoded outcomes must wait. Throughput controls this backlog, while decoding latency also matters for real-time feedforward. Relay-BP is designed for fast, parallel FPGA or ASIC implementations ([Müller et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01779)), and subsequent FPGA work demonstrates real-time gross-code memory decoding ([Maurer et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21600)). Decoding cannot always be deferred until the end because later quantum instructions can depend on earlier measurement outcomes.\n"
msgstr "Dapat prosesuhin ng decoder ang syndrome data nang kasing-bilis man lang ng pagdating nito. Kung hindi, lalaki ang backlog at kailangang maghintay ang mga operasyong umaasa sa mga na-decode na resulta. Ang throughput ang kumokontrol sa backlog na ito, habang mahalaga rin ang decoding latency para sa real-time na feedforward. Ang Relay-BP ay dinisenyo para sa mabibilis at parallel na FPGA o ASIC implementation ([Müller et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.01779)), at ipinapakita ng sumunod na FPGA na gawain ang real-time na gross-code memory decoding ([Maurer et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21600)). Hindi laging maaaring ipagpaliban ang decoding hanggang sa dulo dahil ang mga susunod na quantum instruction ay maaaring umasa sa mga naunang resulta ng measurement.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Part 6: Quantum error correction in the framework of quantum advantage {#part-6-quantum-error-correction-in-the-framework-of-quantum-advantage}"
msgstr "Part 6: Quantum error correction sa balangkas ng quantum advantage {#part-6-quantum-error-correction-in-the-framework-of-quantum-advantage}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658) ground advantage in two criteria. A result must be rigorously *validated*, and it must show a genuine *quantum separation* over classical methods. Operationally, the accuracy spectrum lets us turn those into four concrete questions for any claim:\n"
msgstr "Ibinabatay ng [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658) ang advantage sa dalawang pamantayan. Ang isang resulta ay dapat mahigpit na *na-validate*, at dapat nitong ipakita ang tunay na *quantum separation* kumpara sa mga classical na pamamaraan. Sa praktika, hinahayaan tayo ng accuracy spectrum na gawing apat na konkretong tanong ang mga ito para sa anumang claim:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**What is the task?** Sampling problems, expectation-value computations, search, structured simulation, and analog problems all have different correctness criteria.\n"
msgstr "**Ano ang gawain?** Magkakaiba ang pamantayan ng kawastuhan ng mga sampling problem, expectation-value computation, search, structured simulation, at analog na problema.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**How is correctness validated?** Correctness must be rigorously validated, while performance is assessed against the best available classical methods. Validation can use classical verification, cross-checks between methods, or structural properties such as peakedness in sampling.\n"
msgstr "**Paano vina-validate ang kawastuhan?** Dapat mahigpit na ma-validate ang kawastuhan, habang sinusukat ang performance laban sa pinakamahusay na magagamit na classical na pamamaraan. Maaaring gumamit ang validation ng classical verification, cross-check sa pagitan ng mga pamamaraan, o mga structural na katangian gaya ng peakedness sa sampling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**What resource is paid?** Samples, qubits, decoding compute, runtime, connectivity, calibration time, or classical post-processing. Each tool on the spectrum pays a different resource.\n"
msgstr "**Anong resource ang binabayaran?** Mga sample, qubit, decoding compute, runtime, connectivity, calibration time, o classical post-processing. Bawat tool sa spectrum ay nagbabayad ng ibang resource.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Does QEC preserve or erase the quantum separation after overhead?** Even when QEC works as intended, the overhead it adds might shrink the separation between quantum and classical algorithms more than the algorithmic speedup buys. For deep circuits at stringent logical-error targets, QEC is the standard route to scalable error control. For shallow circuits or near-term advantage windows, suppression-plus-mitigation or post-selected detection might give a better effective separation.\n"
msgstr "**Napapanatili ba o nabubura ng QEC ang quantum separation pagkatapos ng overhead?** Kahit gumagana ang QEC gaya ng inaasahan, ang overhead na idinaragdag nito ay maaaring magpaliit sa separation ng quantum at classical na algorithm nang higit sa nakukuha mula sa algorithmic speedup. Para sa malalalim na circuit na may mahigpit na logical-error target, ang QEC ang karaniwang daan tungo sa scalable na error control. Para sa mababaw na circuit o near-term na advantage window, maaaring magbigay ng mas magandang epektibong separation ang suppression-plus-mitigation o post-selected detection.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*These tools form a resource menu, not a linear recipe*. A real workload chooses a subset of the menu, and the validation-and-separation analysis tells us whether the subset closes the gap to advantage.\n"
msgstr "*Ang mga tool na ito ay bumubuo ng menu ng mga resource, hindi linear na recipe*. Pumipili ang tunay na workload ng subset ng menu, at sinasabi sa atin ng validation-and-separation analysis kung nasasara ng subset ang agwat tungo sa advantage.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The cost crossover {#the-cost-crossover}"
msgstr "Ang cost crossover {#the-cost-crossover}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The cost shapes of different accuracy-spectrum tools are qualitatively different. For the schemes emphasized in earlier lessons, mitigation and post-selected detection can have costs that grow exponentially with circuit volume or total error rate. Sampling overhead in probabilistic error cancellation (PEC) grows roughly as $\\exp(c \\cdot V \\cdot p)$, and post-selected detection acceptance decays roughly as $\\exp(-c' \\cdot V \\cdot p)$, where $V$ is circuit volume and $p$ is physical error rate. This schematic model assumes approximately independent faults, and the constants depend on the protocol and check coverage. Fault-tolerant correction with decoding has a different shape. It pays a large upfront architectural overhead (code patches, magic-state factories, decoders, and modular hardware), followed by more controlled scaling with circuit volume once logical error rates are suppressed. These cost shapes are schematic, with the exact crossover point depending on physical error rates, target logical fidelity, and the specific algorithm. The qualitative point is that mitigation and detection can be the right choice at smaller volumes, while correction can become favorable once circuit volume and reliability targets make those lighter-weight approaches too costly. The crossover defines the *regime* in which any advantage claim sits.\n"
msgstr "Magkaiba sa kalidad ang hugis ng gastos ng iba't ibang tool sa accuracy spectrum. Para sa mga scheme na binigyang-diin sa mga naunang lesson, ang mitigation at post-selected detection ay maaaring may mga gastos na lumalaki nang exponential kasabay ng circuit volume o kabuuang error rate. Ang sampling overhead sa probabilistic error cancellation (PEC) ay lumalaki nang humigit-kumulang bilang $\\exp(c \\cdot V \\cdot p)$, at ang acceptance ng post-selected detection ay bumababa nang humigit-kumulang bilang $\\exp(-c' \\cdot V \\cdot p)$, kung saan ang $V$ ay circuit volume at ang $p$ ay physical error rate. Ipinapalagay ng schematic na modelong ito ang mga halos independent na fault, at ang mga constant ay nakadepende sa protocol at sa saklaw ng check. Ang fault-tolerant correction na may decoding ay may ibang hugis. Nagbabayad ito ng malaking paunang architectural overhead (mga code patch, magic-state factory, decoder, at modular na hardware), na sinusundan ng mas kontroladong pag-scale kasabay ng circuit volume kapag napigilan na ang mga logical error rate. Schematic lang ang mga hugis ng gastos na ito, at nakadepende ang eksaktong crossover point sa mga physical error rate, target na logical fidelity, at partikular na algorithm. Ang punto sa kalidad ay na ang mitigation at detection ay maaaring tamang pagpili sa mas maliliit na volume, habang ang correction ay maaaring maging mas paborable kapag ang circuit volume at mga target sa reliability ay ginawang masyadong magastos ang mga mas magaan na pamamaraang iyon. Tinutukoy ng crossover ang *regime* kung saan nakalagay ang anumang advantage claim.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For near-term advantage demonstrations, mitigation and post-selection can be useful when their cost stays manageable at the volumes current hardware can run. For large-scale fault-tolerant computation, decoding-based correction becomes central because the overhead of rigorous mitigation or post-selected detection alone can become prohibitive. These methods can also be combined along the QEC continuum (see the [QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)).\n"
msgstr "Para sa mga near-term na advantage demonstration, magagamit ang mitigation at post-selection kapag nananatiling kayang pamahalaan ang gastos nila sa mga volume na kayang patakbuhin ng kasalukuyang hardware. Para sa malakihang fault-tolerant computation, nagiging sentral ang decoding-based correction dahil ang overhead ng mahigpit na mitigation o post-selected detection lang ay maaaring maging napakataas. Maaari ding pagsamahin ang mga pamamaraang ito sa kahabaan ng QEC continuum (tingnan ang [QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-5}"
msgstr "Suriin ang iyong pag-unawa {#check-your-understanding-5}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the schematic model above, PEC, post-selection, and fault-tolerant correction with decoding have *qualitatively different* cost shapes as functions of circuit volume $V$. Describe each shape, and what it implies for when each tool wins.\n"
msgstr "Sa schematic na modelo sa itaas, ang PEC, post-selection, at fault-tolerant correction na may decoding ay may *magkaibang hugis* ng gastos bilang mga function ng circuit volume $V$. Ilarawan ang bawat hugis, at kung ano ang ipinahihiwatig nito kung kailan nananalo ang bawat tool.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this model, PEC and post-selection have exponential costs in volume, with PEC sampling overhead $\\sim e^{cVp}$ and post-selection acceptance $\\sim e^{-c'Vp}$. Fault-tolerant correction with decoding instead pays a large *upfront* architectural cost (code patches, magic-state factories, decoders, modular hardware) and then grows *gently* once logical error rates are suppressed. The relative costs can cross. Small circuits favor mitigation and detection, deep circuits favor correction, and that crossover defines the *regime* of any advantage claim. (Real cost models are messier, and the robust point is the difference in shape, not an exact crossing. Note that PEC and post-selection costs in this model are *not* flat in $V$, since their blow-up is the whole point, and correction is specifically *not* exponential in $V$.)\n"
msgstr "Sa modelong ito, ang PEC at post-selection ay may exponential na gastos sa volume, kung saan ang PEC sampling overhead ay $\\sim e^{cVp}$ at ang post-selection acceptance ay $\\sim e^{-c'Vp}$. Ang fault-tolerant correction na may decoding naman ay nagbabayad ng malaking *paunang* architectural na gastos (mga code patch, magic-state factory, decoder, modular na hardware) at saka lumalaki nang *marahan* kapag napigilan na ang mga logical error rate. Maaaring magtagpo ang magkakaibang gastos. Pabor sa mitigation at detection ang maliliit na circuit, pabor sa correction ang malalalim na circuit, at ang crossover na iyon ang tumutukoy sa *regime* ng anumang advantage claim. (Mas magulo ang mga tunay na modelo ng gastos, at ang matibay na punto ay ang pagkakaiba sa hugis, hindi ang eksaktong tagpuan. Tandaan na ang mga gastos ng PEC at post-selection sa modelong ito ay *hindi* patag sa $V$, dahil ang pagsabog nila ang buong punto, at ang correction ay partikular na *hindi* exponential sa $V$.)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Where this leaves the \"when\" question {#where-this-leaves-the-when-question}"
msgstr "Saan ito nag-iiwan sa tanong na \"kailan\" {#where-this-leaves-the-when-question}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The honest endpoint is not that correction *wins*, or that mitigation or detection wins, but that we can weigh *how well each tool fits a given advantage claim* against the following factors: the task, how correctness is validated, and the resource budget. The case study in Parts 3-5 is one concrete attempt to push the correction end of the spectrum until that cost crossover moves in its favor, and it is still moving. The most recent step, *code concatenation*, builds a larger code by encoding the logical qubits of an *inner* code into the data slots of an *outer* code. This relaxes the overhead-suppression-connectivity trade-off one notch further by supplying non-locality through the inner code's *logical* operations rather than through hardware. It is reported to reach the teraquop regime for memory with lower space overhead than the 288-data-qubit two-gross code in the authors' analysis ([Wills et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21898)). The large-scale BB architecture is still supported mainly by circuit-level simulations and architectural analysis. Separately, IBM and partners reported quantum-advantage demonstrations on 30 July 2026 ([quantum-advantage announcement](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/quantum-advantage)), with ongoing comparisons against classical methods through the [Quantum Advantage Tracker](https://quantum-advantage-tracker.github.io/). The experiment by IBM and the University of Chicago used spacetime-code post-selection, the approach introduced in the previous lesson, and reported roughly a $10\\times$ reduction in effective gate error ([quantum-advantage announcement](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/quantum-advantage)). Its first 70-qubit instance was later simulated classically ([Manabe et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13110)), and the revised experiment uses a 64-qubit circuit encoded in 76 physical qubits ([Martiel et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25941)). Starling's large-scale fault-tolerance milestone remains a 2029 target ([fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc)). This matches what [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658) anticipate, with advantage arriving not as a single milestone but as a *sequence* of increasingly robust demonstrations, each validated against the best classical baseline and settled in the open.\n"
msgstr "Ang tapat na konklusyon ay hindi na *nananalo* ang correction, o na nananalo ang mitigation o detection, kundi na maititimbang natin *kung gaano kaangkop ang bawat tool sa isang partikular na advantage claim* laban sa mga sumusunod na salik: ang gawain, kung paano vina-validate ang kawastuhan, at ang budget ng resource. Ang case study sa Parts 3-5 ay isang konkretong pagtatangkang itulak ang dulo ng correction sa spectrum hanggang sa lumipat sa panig nito ang cost crossover, at patuloy pa itong gumagalaw. Ang pinakabagong hakbang, ang *code concatenation*, ay bumubuo ng mas malaking code sa pamamagitan ng pag-encode ng mga logical qubit ng isang *inner* code sa mga data slot ng isang *outer* code. Pinaluluwag nito nang isang antas pa ang trade-off ng overhead-suppression-connectivity sa pamamagitan ng pagbibigay ng non-locality sa pamamagitan ng mga *logical* na operasyon ng inner code sa halip na sa hardware. Iniulat itong umaabot sa teraquop regime para sa memory na may mas mababang space overhead kaysa sa 288-data-qubit na two-gross code sa pagsusuri ng mga may-akda ([Wills et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21898)). Ang malakihang BB architecture ay pangunahing sinusuportahan pa rin ng mga circuit-level na simulation at architectural analysis. Hiwalay dito, nag-ulat ang IBM at mga katuwang ng mga quantum-advantage demonstration noong 30 Hulyo 2026 ([anunsyo ng quantum-advantage](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/quantum-advantage)), na may patuloy na paghahambing sa mga classical na pamamaraan sa pamamagitan ng [Quantum Advantage Tracker](https://quantum-advantage-tracker.github.io/). Ang eksperimento ng IBM at University of Chicago ay gumamit ng spacetime-code post-selection, ang approach na ipinakilala sa nakaraang lesson, at nag-ulat ng humigit-kumulang $10\\times$ na pagbaba sa epektibong gate error ([anunsyo ng quantum-advantage](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/quantum-advantage)). Ang una nitong 70-qubit na instance ay na-simulate kalaunan sa classical na paraan ([Manabe et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13110)), at ang binagong eksperimento ay gumagamit ng 64-qubit na circuit na naka-encode sa 76 na pisikal na qubit ([Martiel et al., 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.25941)). Nananatiling 2029 na target ang large-scale fault-tolerance milestone ng Starling ([fault-tolerance roadmap](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/large-scale-ftqc)). Tugma ito sa inaasahan ng [Lanes et al., 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20658), kung saan ang advantage ay dumarating hindi bilang iisang milestone kundi bilang *sunud-sunod* na lalong matitibay na demonstrasyon, bawat isa ay na-validate laban sa pinakamahusay na classical baseline at napagpasyahan nang hayagan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Evaluating new results {#evaluating-new-results}"
msgstr "Pagsusuri ng mga bagong resulta {#evaluating-new-results}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing an error-handling strategy starts with the task, how correctness will be validated, and the full resource cost. The code, decoder, and hardware examples in this lesson help assess whether error correction can meet those requirements while preserving a computational advantage. Mitigation and post-selection can complement error correction, including on logical circuits ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)).\n"
msgstr "Nagsisimula ang pagpili ng estratehiya sa paghawak ng error sa gawain, kung paano vina-validate ang kawastuhan, at sa buong gastos ng resource. Ang mga halimbawa ng code, decoder, at hardware sa lesson na ito ay tumutulong suriin kung natutugunan ng error correction ang mga requirement na iyon habang pinapanatili ang computational advantage. Maaaring dagdagan ng mitigation at post-selection ang error correction, kasama sa mga logical circuit ([QEC continuum blog](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/qec-continuum)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The [Quantum Advantage Tracker](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/quantum-advantage-tracker) supports open comparison of candidate quantum computations with leading classical methods. As both improve, performance comparisons and resource estimates need to be revisited. For each new result, ask what accuracy is demonstrated, which resources are counted, and whether the comparison uses the best available classical methods. Use these questions when you assess or design an error-handling workflow.\n"
msgstr "Sinusuportahan ng [Quantum Advantage Tracker](https://www.ibm.com/quantum/blog/quantum-advantage-tracker) ang bukas na paghahambing ng mga kandidatong quantum computation sa nangungunang classical na pamamaraan. Habang gumagaling ang dalawa, kailangang suriing muli ang mga paghahambing ng performance at mga estimate ng resource. Para sa bawat bagong resulta, itanong kung anong katumpakan ang ipinapakita, aling mga resource ang binibilang, at kung ginagamit ng paghahambing ang pinakamahusay na magagamit na classical na pamamaraan. Gamitin ang mga tanong na ito kapag sinusuri o dinidisenyo mo ang isang workflow sa paghawak ng error.\n"

